{"offset": 0, "next": 100, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c9b9cd5e0b6447a180914049754459b00b3cf264", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1016/j.cobeha.2026.101684", "CorpusId": 292192120}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c9b9cd5e0b6447a180914049754459b00b3cf264", "title": "Self-generated goals in the age of foundation models: artificial autotelic agents and their implications for cognitive science", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cobeha.2026.101684", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1016/j.cobeha.2026.101684?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cobeha.2026.101684, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-12-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2303653748", "name": "Gaia Molinaro"}, {"authorId": "2238473833", "name": "Anne G. E. Collins"}, {"authorId": "1720664", "name": "P. Oudeyer"}, {"authorId": "102281182", "name": "C\u00e9dric Colas"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8cc760d359b67956686aecabb7e893fffaa9b4ea", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1016/j.entcom.2026.101258", "CorpusId": 294335405}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8cc760d359b67956686aecabb7e893fffaa9b4ea", "title": "From world model capability to continued play intention: The mediating role of perceived NPC intelligence and the moderating role of anthropomorphism", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": "CLOSED", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1016/j.entcom.2026.101258?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcom.2026.101258, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-12-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2260298114", "name": "Youngkeun Choi"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "33e84e388ed32372fb1a7fbbc251f96f3375a29a", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1016/j.chaos.2026.119312", "CorpusId": 295339373}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/33e84e388ed32372fb1a7fbbc251f96f3375a29a", "title": "From observation to generation: A complex-systems perspective on generative Human\u2013AI social systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": "CLOSED", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1016/j.chaos.2026.119312?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chaos.2026.119312, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-12-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2326993006", "name": "Yi-Ping Ma"}, {"authorId": "2324724597", "name": "Meng-Yao Peng"}, {"authorId": "2364574710", "name": "Shi-Yu Hu"}, {"authorId": "2386950668", "name": "Bu-Yuan Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2269423169", "name": "Yi-Pei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2374910389", "name": "Yu-Qing Chen"}, {"authorId": "2328049007", "name": "Shi-Qing Liu"}, {"authorId": "2685641", "name": "K. Cheong"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7872d98d7366afe3ac62a134eb74aa3efdf3018b", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1109/JIOT.2026.3729453", "CorpusId": 291669126}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7872d98d7366afe3ac62a134eb74aa3efdf3018b", "title": "Brain Cognition-Inspired Adaptable LLM Agents for Continuous Knowledge Reuse Across Tasks in IoT", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/jiot.2026.3729453", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/JIOT.2026.3729453?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/JIOT.2026.3729453, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-11-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2275783305", "name": "Tao Chen"}, {"authorId": "2399684758", "name": "Meng-Meng Sui"}, {"authorId": "2275889164", "name": "Bao-Yu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2282281130", "name": "Shao-Hua Cao"}, {"authorId": "2108238370", "name": "Wei-Shan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2166818520", "name": "Lian-Yong Qi"}, {"authorId": "2271845504", "name": "Xiao-Li Li"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM)-based agents are promising in Internet of Things (IoT) applications. However, existing agent systems suffer from an ephemeral limitation: agents are instantiated for individual tasks and terminated upon completion, losing valuable execution experiences across different tasks where past knowledge reuse is impossible. Inspired by dual-process theory in cognitive science, we propose a brain cognition-inspired dual-system agent framework for continuous knowledge reuse across heterogeneous task streams, termed the cognition-inspired dual-system agent framework for continuous knowledge reuse (CIA-CKR). The CIA-CKR framework separates fast (reactive execution) from slow (deliberative planning) cognition phases by maintaining a persistent knowledge base throughout the operational lifetime of agents. Two synergistic mechanisms are designed that mirror bidirectional knowledge flow in human cognition: a personalized agent generation mechanism that retrieves task-relevant knowledge and configures context-specific agent capabilities through top-down adaptation, and a knowledge accumulation mechanism that extracts experiential insights from execution traces through bottom-up feedback. Extensive experiments on HomeBench (smart home control) and OmniEAR (embodied multiagent collaboration) datasets demonstrate that CIA-CKR consistently outperforms both vanilla LLMs and recent memory-augmented methods, achieving 10.82% and 4.23% average accuracy improvements on HomeBench and OmniEAR, respectively, over the best baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b8a7f0d7b4bdb20292802143a858e9d2dc96e6ed", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.12068", "CorpusId": 295732419}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b8a7f0d7b4bdb20292802143a858e9d2dc96e6ed", "title": "MIRIX-2.0: Multi-Agent Memory System", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12068, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-09", "authors": [{"authorId": null, "name": "Wei-Chieh Huang"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Wei-Zhi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2376539245", "name": "Jia-Yan Nan"}, {"authorId": "2261083308", "name": "Peng Xia"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Yu Wang"}], "abstract": "Long-term memory lets LLM agents personalize, stay consistent, and reuse what worked before. Parametric memories need training, consolidation can lose details for good, and keeping every record leaves a growing store to search. We present MIRIX-2.0, a multi-agent memory system designed to be practical, accurate, and efficient at once. It is the most accurate of the compared systems on four of five QA benchmarks. It reaches 91.4% on LoCoMo and 72.6% on LongMemEval-S, where it leads the strongest baseline by 14.3 points, and with AutoDream disabled it gains 31.0 and 22.0 points over HyperMem on single-hop and multi-hop document QA. As procedural memory, it yields the highest mean success among the compared memory methods on ALFWorld and WebShop and reaches 49.2% on MetaClaw-Bench, against 44.0% for MetaClaw's skill library. Six typed memory agents keep complete records as the system of record, and a provenance hypergraph indexes episodic and semantic records with n-ary frames that cite their rows. Retrieval runs under explicit budgets, so the evidence a question inspects does not grow with the history. AutoDream merges duplicate anchors outside the write path without rewriting any record, and it distills task sessions into skills. With AutoDream disabled, MIRIX-2.0 answers faster than HyperMem on all five timed benchmarks. It needs no fine-tuning, runs with closed and open models, and its code is public."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0c0a14c0a73dfddc033f6edbb65628184f16d4b8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.12120", "CorpusId": 295732090}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0c0a14c0a73dfddc033f6edbb65628184f16d4b8", "title": "SCALE: Small-Model-First Concept Abstraction with LLM-Guided Experience Internalization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12120, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-09", "authors": [{"authorId": null, "name": "Hong Su"}], "abstract": "Autonomous agents must continually acquire reusable knowledge from experience without relying on costly large language model (LLM) reasoning for every decision. However, existing experience-based approaches often struggle to transform accumulated observations and reasoning into compact knowledge that lightweight models can independently apply. This paper proposes SCALE, a small-model-first framework that integrates selective LLM reasoning, experience-driven concept abstraction, and knowledge internalization. A lightweight model handles routine predictions, while prediction errors and accumulated cross-episode evidence selectively trigger LLM reflection. The LLM analyzes relevant historical experiences, generates positive and negative counterfactual examples, and proposes reusable concept hypotheses. Independently validated concepts are progressively incorporated into hierarchical neural representations, enabling lightweight models to exploit increasingly abstract knowledge. Meanwhile, mastered experiences are excluded from active memory while their original records remain available in a complete archive. Experiments across ten random seeds show that historical recall improves held-out accuracy from 87.11% to 98.28%, hierarchical concept knowledge increases difficult-case accuracy from 55.33% to 66.47%, and abstract archiving reduces active experience records from 6,000 to 1,528."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0c9858a786b99982223249bb2f59898ac930df4d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.12885", "CorpusId": 295732748}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0c9858a786b99982223249bb2f59898ac930df4d", "title": "World In-Context Learning for Agentic Robot Manipulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12885, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2482899113", "name": "Hen-Shao Fang"}, {"authorId": "2529816717", "name": "Ze-Hui Guo"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Yue-Han Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2353582170", "name": "Ming-Yang Du"}, {"authorId": "94882716", "name": "Ding-Kang Liang"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Xiang Bai"}], "abstract": "Trained action policies write manipulation skill into network weights, tying what is learned to the distribution it is learned from. In-context learning (ICL) avoids this coupling but has not matched trained policies, because a demonstration recorded in another scene carries no feedback from the world the robot currently faces. We observe that what an agent learns in context is semantic, recording task steps and object roles rather than visual appearance, so experience gathered in one world transfers to another. Based on this insight, we propose World In-Context Learning (WICL), a paradigm in which a frozen model starts from a demonstration, rehearses the task in a world such as a simulator, and writes the feedback into its context rather than its weights. We build RoboScript, a modular infrastructure that implements WICL for agentic manipulation. From a single demonstration, RoboScript learns each task through rehearsal in simulation, freezes the learned knowledge as a Task Memory, and deploys the frozen memory on new scenes with data isolation that ensures every deployment input is available on a real robot. On all 50 tasks of RoboTwin 2.0, RoboScript reaches 83.4% mean success across clean and randomized settings with one demonstration per task and no gradient updates, exceeding the strongest trained action policy by over 20 points. The method generalizes across visual distributions, improves through reflection, scales with the foundation model, and transfers to a physical dual-arm robot through cross-embodiment memory adaptation. With a world in the loop, a robot can learn a manipulation task in its context as well as it learns one in its weights. All code is available at https://github.com/ArcheOne-AI/RoboScript"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f3ea0e80b863665994a3b0a9b4519e014292fd29", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.11509", "CorpusId": 295731653}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ea0e80b863665994a3b0a9b4519e014292fd29", "title": "Review the Episodes, Refine the Lessons: Simple Memory Evolution for Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11509, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-08", "authors": [{"authorId": null, "name": "Yi-Fei Yao"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Yu-De Zou"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Xing Gao"}, {"authorId": "2329820992", "name": "Guan-Bo Jiao"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Yu-Kai Wang"}, {"authorId": "2328977598", "name": "Jian-Ming Ma"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Wei-Nan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2257475525", "name": "Chun-Hua Shen"}], "abstract": "Recent studies of embodied agents based on frontier multimodal large language models (MLLMs) develop harness engineering around the MLLM, such as harnesses with post-trained policies and recursive self-improvement loops. Yet whether these additions exceed the native capability of the MLLM remains unexplored. We introduce SimplEvo to explore how far an MLLM can improve under direct control when it retains its execution experience in self-written text memories. It requires no weight updates, post-trained policies, or privileged information. The MLLM writes a coordinate-free task memory once from a single demonstration. Under a strict train-test protocol, it then creates a bounded working memory on training episodes only and refines it after each episode with lessons on procedure, completion, and recovery. Both memories stay fixed at test time. On EBench, SimplEvo lifts the success rate (SR) of GPT-6-Astra under direct control from 46.7% to 81.7%, and tops the RoboDojo leaderboard with 69.63% SR and a score of 75.32. Compared with memories that also store past visual frames, SimplEvo reaches a similar SR with text memory alone while using fewer tokens. With its memories fixed, SimplEvo also generalizes to unseen layouts and randomized scene configurations. Moreover, the working memory transfers across models: written by GPT-6-Astra, it can be reused as is by weaker MLLMs and improve their performance without any adaptation of their own."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cb322a988f9a7284a37dda53d04d19add0232a26", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.11565", "CorpusId": 295732315}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cb322a988f9a7284a37dda53d04d19add0232a26", "title": "Actions Should EARN Their Credit: Test-Time Adaptation and Route Consolidation for Frozen LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11565, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-08", "authors": [{"authorId": null, "name": "Bian Sun"}, {"authorId": "2383302368", "name": "Kevin Zhai"}, {"authorId": "2349243540", "name": "Mubarak Shah"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Song Wang"}], "abstract": "LLM agents can use feedback from repeated attempts to improve their decisions without updating model parameters. However, positive action credits can continue to favor actions that repeatedly achieve the same score, even when higher scores are achievable. Under a fixed interaction budget, trying new actions in the final attempts can also lower performance. We introduce EARN, a method that uses past experience to guide further progress and retain earlier performance through conditional reuse of effective action sequences. EARN reduces action credits for reaching scores repeatedly attained in earlier episodes and assigns larger credits to action segments that reach higher scores. These credits adjust the probabilities of LLM-proposed actions. During the final episodes, EARN directly replays stored actions when the current context matches the recorded context and the actions remain available, bypassing LLM candidate generation. We evaluate performance at the end of the interaction budget using the average score over the final four episodes. Across Jericho, ScienceWorld, and Crafter, EARN achieves the best score on this metric in 31 of 33 task--budget settings among the evaluated methods, including ties. Across the evaluated Jericho budgets, replay bypasses 78.9--86.3\\% of candidate-generation calls in the final four episodes, averaged across games."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fa697043ee4674d0f4c5dc1a995f98402181ec10", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10501", "CorpusId": 295730615}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fa697043ee4674d0f4c5dc1a995f98402181ec10", "title": "Sampling Is Not Curiosity: Why LLM Agents Should Investigate", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10501, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2039956094", "name": "Alfonso Amayuelas"}, {"authorId": "2278443843", "name": "P. Piekos"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Xin Eric Wang"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "William Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2311440772", "name": "Jurgen Schmidhuber"}, {"authorId": "2280557520", "name": "A. Pentland"}], "abstract": "LLM agents are increasingly used for open-ended tasks, very common in research scenarios where they are required to explore, investigate, or act curiously. At inference time, an LLM is a fixed map from context to next-token distribution, the pretrained weights determine the function, and only its conditioning changes as context accumulates. Mechanisms like temperature, best-of-N, tree search, or RAG vary how the distribution is sampled or what it is conditioned on, but do not update the agent's model of the environment. We argue that current LLM agents do not explore curiously; they explore stochastically. From how curiosity is understood in the RL literature, it requires a predictive structure over how the environment responds to the agent's actions, which improves online from current interaction, drives action selection through its predicted improvement, and persists beyond the context window. Additionally, in its open-ended form, the agent should intrinsically originate what to investigate. Thus, we propose a framework defining curiosity in LLM agents, reserving the term for systems that satisfy it. In doing so, we identify the research directions its implementation opens up."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1e662f8b344d526862d397421e9e19426b662e72", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10542", "CorpusId": 295731241}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1e662f8b344d526862d397421e9e19426b662e72", "title": "When Experience Becomes a Backdoor: Poisoning Self-Evolving LLM Agents via Failure-Aware Task Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10542, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-07", "authors": [{"authorId": null, "name": "Min-Hua Lin"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Zong-Yu Wu"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Han-Qing Lu"}, {"authorId": "2313288320", "name": "Xian-Feng Tang"}, {"authorId": "2254295097", "name": "Xiang Zhang"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Qi He"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Su-Hang Wang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly improve themselves through self-evolution, distilling execution experience into persistent, reusable skills. However, this persistence creates a new attack surface: malicious rules implanted into skills can compromise subsequent agent behavior. Existing skill backdoor attacks typically assume direct manipulation of the victim's skills, which is impractical when skills are constructed internally by a trusted evolver. In this paper, we study a novel problem of task-level backdoor attacks, where the attacker can only inject poisoned evolution tasks, inducing the agent to implant backdoors into its own skills. Since the attacker controls neither skill induction nor downstream execution, a natural approach is to iteratively optimize poisoned tasks based on attack success. However, our preliminary analysis reveals two key challenges: (i) failures at different stages of skill evolution and downstream execution can produce indistinguishable outcomes, providing little guidance for targeted poisoned-task updates; and (ii) unsuccessful updates may undermine earlier progress, while simply discarding them may lead to repeated ineffective updates. To address these challenges, we propose BadEvolve, a failure-aware task-poisoning framework against self-evolving LLM agents. BadEvolve diagnoses attack failures across four stages, i.e., Induction, Activation, Following, and Effect, and directs updates toward the failing stage. It further employs rollback to preserve earlier progress and failure memory to avoid repeating ineffective updates. Extensive experiments across diverse self-evolution frameworks, benchmarks, and backbone LLMs demonstrate the effectiveness of BadEvolve. Our code is available at https://github.com/ventr1c/BadEvolve."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "90923886ba0f826dbc7eecc2d6aa2f91cbd99417", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10748", "CorpusId": 295731453}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/90923886ba0f826dbc7eecc2d6aa2f91cbd99417", "title": "REINS: A Self-Evolving Agent Harness for Real-Time Trajectory Planning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10748, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2089582898", "name": "Zhi-Hong Cui"}, {"authorId": "2353364375", "name": "Heng-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2294629231", "name": "Hao-Ran Tang"}, {"authorId": "2131145119", "name": "Shi-Jun Liu"}, {"authorId": "2848485", "name": "Amirhosein Taherkordi"}, {"authorId": "2341325984", "name": "Torkel Skeie"}], "abstract": "Agent harnesses -- runtime scaffolds that wrap an LLM's reasoning loop with tool dispatch, verification, and persistent memory -- have become the standard substrate for deploying LLMs as autonomous agents, as demonstrated by Claude Code and Codex in software engineering. In trajectory planning, however, this paradigm has not taken hold: existing LLM-based trajectory planning methods either keep the LLM in the decision loop, exceeding the millisecond-level control budget, or offload it to offline rules or training-time supervision -- leaving the runtime planner non-evolving. Intermediate hybrids still lack the verification-and-consolidation loop that makes harnesses reliable. We ask: what runtime harness would let an LLM planner meet real-time constraints while self-evolving into a deployable one? We propose REINS, a harness built on three LLM-planner couplings: LLM outputs are (i) grounded to a learned variable-level dynamics model, (ii) verified via calibrated predicates and forward rollout, and (iii) consolidated into an indexed rule memory that serves future scenes in O(log n) without re-invoking the LLM. The harness closes a self-evolution loop: familiar scenes resolve by memory lookup; novel scenes invoke the LLM, whose verified outputs enrich the memory. Without modifying LLM weights, REINS attains 98%+ compliance, nearly 50% collision reduction, and 1.9-4.6 ms latency on simulated and real-world benchmarks at the tactical decision layer, with LLM invocation rate monotonically declining as memory matures. Code: https://github.com/ZhihongCui/REINS"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8cc12be3fa5d57fededdac988039f4f9bd83c274", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10510", "CorpusId": 295733144}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8cc12be3fa5d57fededdac988039f4f9bd83c274", "title": "ReSkill: Selective Self-Improvement for Robot Manipulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10510, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2307394858", "name": "Zheng Sun"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Zhi-Hao Li"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Zhuo Li"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Wen-Rui Liu"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Xin Wang"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Fei Chen"}], "abstract": "Self-improvement on compositional manipulation tasks requires deciding what to reuse, where to improve, and which changes to retain. We present ReSkill, a selective self-improvement framework that concentrates revision on capability gaps. A coding agent constructs an object-centric task graph from an instruction and a skill library, then uses node-level execution records to locate failures. At the task level, it revises stage organization, hand-off conditions, and recovery routes; at the skill level, it strengthens perception, motion, manipulation, and verification capabilities that repeatedly fail. Candidate revisions undergo full-task evaluation and paired comparisons before adoption, allowing local changes to accumulate into validated skills and an independently executable policy. The agent participates only during development; the deployed policy calls the retained skills without invoking the agent at run time. On the same development set, average success across ten RoboDojo tasks rises from 30.0% for the initial versions to 86.2% for the final adopted programs; retained programs achieve 83.6% in recorded robustness and model-selection evaluations. Across eight real-world tasks, ReSkill succeeds in 72 of 80 rebuilt-simulation trials and 62 of 80 real-robot trials (77.5%). The median revision changes only three functions, approximately 0.5% of a program, while 39% of task-specific functions in later-added tasks directly reuse existing implementations. These results show how locating local deficiencies, validating candidate changes, and accumulating reusable skills can improve execution of compositional manipulation tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7c480cb7ebb82bd921613568e2be1f1b75e94f9e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10477", "CorpusId": 295732302}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7c480cb7ebb82bd921613568e2be1f1b75e94f9e", "title": "PatternBloom: Empowering Agentic RAG with Externalized RL-Distilled Graph Patterns", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10477, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2412748808", "name": "Yi-Rou Ge"}, {"authorId": "2391566480", "name": "Heng Huang"}], "abstract": "Reinforcement-learning-trained agents for retrieval-augmented generation retrieve content (passages, knowledge-graph fragments, or hyperedges) and supervise training with outcome rewards on the final answer. Across these paradigms the reasoning route through retrieved content never leaves the language model's hidden state, conflating declarative knowledge with the procedural knowledge that recurs across queries of the same logical type; content-only methods consequently plateau as reasoning depth grows. We introduce PatternBloom, an agentic RAG framework that shifts the unit of retrieval from content (what to read) to logic (how to reason). A first RL stage scores the agent's constructed evidence graph by a frozen oracle's information gain through the Information-Density Reward (IDR), a size-normalized mutual-information surrogate. High-reward trajectories are distilled into a Graph Pattern Memory (GPM) of type-abstracted reasoning skeletons, and a second RL stage uses the Pattern-Augmented Reward (PAR) to couple policy to memory, turning procedural reasoning into a learnable, externalized structural prior. A 7B PatternBloom outperforms $27$ baselines by $+15.0$ Avg-OOD EM, with the gain compounding with reasoning depth precisely because procedural knowledge, once distilled, is reused rather than re-derived at every query. We release our code at https://github.com/EllenYiGe/PatternBloom."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "61e3aac6cf6823f34b1e02154a88461b119c8494", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10787", "CorpusId": 295731441}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/61e3aac6cf6823f34b1e02154a88461b119c8494", "title": "Trustworthy Recursive Self-Improvement with Statistical Guarantees", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10787, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Mathematics", "Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-07", "authors": [{"authorId": null, "name": "Zi-He Gao"}, {"authorId": "2283261562", "name": "Zheng-Ling Qi"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Yi-Fan Cui"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement offers a promising path toward AI agents that improve through experience by refining their own harnesses without retraining their underlying large language models (LLMs). Making this self-improvement process reliable, however, requires more than generating new updates: agents must decide which changes are worth keeping. A greedy gate that accepts updates based solely on higher validation scores can mistake an observed gain for genuine progress and lead to performance degeneration, whereas overly conservative statistical gates may reject beneficial updates and hinder performance improvement. We introduce Trustworthy Recursive Updates with Statistical Testing (TRUST), a framework that adapts an online multiple hypothesis testing procedure to control the rate of false evolution across rounds, with an LLM analyzing historical context to help determine how much evidence is required to update the incumbent. Under interpretable assumptions, we prove that TRUST limits the proportion of accepted updates that result in unacceptable performance loss. We further derive a lower bound on the final performance gain. Across the benchmarks HotpotQA, DROP, and APPS with the models DeepSeek-V4-Flash and Qwen-3.8-Flash, TRUST reduces the average ratio of accepted inferior updates from the greedy gate's 45.59% to 2.78%, while achieving higher final scores than the state-of-the-art statistical gate baselines for recursive self-improvement. These findings demonstrate that TRUST strikes a balance between statistical reliability and performance gain in recursive self-improvement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ab5db10f31bc3d03cd45204cfeadd30aaa8632e4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.08865", "CorpusId": 295660182}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ab5db10f31bc3d03cd45204cfeadd30aaa8632e4", "title": "Rethinking Agent Trajectory Compression: A Workflow-Aware Perspective", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08865, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2397486819", "name": "Qi-Jia Zhuang"}, {"authorId": "2519784872", "name": "Rui-Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Wen-Qi Hu"}, {"authorId": "2327010861", "name": "Yu-Xiang Ren"}], "abstract": "As long-horizon agents increasingly rely on multi-step tool interactions to accomplish complex tasks, their execution trajectories continuously accumulate structured outputs and intermediate artifacts, leading to growing context overhead. Existing compression methods primarily rely on text importance or fixed truncation rules, often overlooking cross-step dependencies within workflows and potentially discarding critical information required for subsequent task execution. To address this challenge, we propose WAAC, a workflow-aware framework for agent trajectory compression. WAAC structurally models successful historical trajectories to extract reusable workflow compression experience and incorporates online backward dependency analysis to dynamically identify information retention requirements. By combining current dependency constraints with historical experience, WAAC selects appropriate compression strategies for different types of historical information. Experiments on PinchBench and Odysseys demonstrate that WAAC achieves an overall score of 87.6 and a perfect completion rate of 19.0%, respectively, while reducing execution costs by 19.7% and 29.1% compared with the strongest baseline methods. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of workflow-aware trajectory compression in improving task performance and cost efficiency for long-horizon agents. Code and resources are available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/waac-6527/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2e3e78dc87deb8e70bc64705e5dc37c486cc7ff2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09454", "CorpusId": 295660213}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2e3e78dc87deb8e70bc64705e5dc37c486cc7ff2", "title": "From LoRA to Language: Recovering Skill Documents from Adapters", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09454, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2528585813", "name": "Saranya Dintakurthy Swadha"}, {"authorId": "2316767102", "name": "Pavan C. Shekar"}, {"authorId": "2310396701", "name": "Arpit Jain"}, {"authorId": "2294567922", "name": "Aswanth Krishnan"}], "abstract": "Agent skills are short documents that teach a language model how to handle a class of tasks. To save context, these documents are increasingly compiled into low-rank adapters (LoRA), which keep the behaviour but lose the text: an adapter is hard to read, edit, audit or move to another model. We ask whether the skill document can be written back from the adapter and its frozen base model, and we score each recovered document by the share of the original's key instructions it states and by the tasks it solves. Reading the weights alone recovered little with the methods we tried; a learned map from weights to text passed on about one bit of the document. Running the adapter worked much better. The Executing Writer, a small writer trained on top of the adapted model, recovers 39-44% of the key instructions of held-out skills, against 1.3-1.5% with another skill's adapter, and on adapters fitted to 41 external skills its documents restore 85-91% of the original's task gain. These strongest results are on adapters whose training stored the document; adapters trained on behaviour alone are much harder for trained writers (1-2% of instructions). For these adapters we test an agent that runs the base and adapted models and writes a skill from where they differ. On ALFWorld its skills recover 58-71% of a LoRA's gain and carry over to models that cannot load the LoRA, lifting Llama-3.1-8B from 5% to 55%. Whether such a skill comes from the adapter depends on what the adapter learned: when its behaviour cannot be guessed, the adapter is the source; when it can, a reward loop without the adapter does as well."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b1bb4ada8f1114ccf7a9e2774b3d0f0beedc7609", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09318", "CorpusId": 295660338}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b1bb4ada8f1114ccf7a9e2774b3d0f0beedc7609", "title": "Recursively Self-Improving Electromagnetic Spectrum Agents: Architecture and Operation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09318, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2219241939", "name": "Bao-Lin Chong"}, {"authorId": "2493431844", "name": "Dongfang Xu"}, {"authorId": "90744463", "name": "Qiao-Si Wang"}, {"authorId": "2114902865", "name": "Fu-Hui Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2259827557", "name": "Qi-Hui Wu"}], "abstract": "Electromagnetic spectrum agents (ESAs) must contend with changing environments and task requirements during sustained autonomous operation, yet their existing capabilities may not remain suitable for new operating conditions. This article proposes recursively self-improving electromagnetic spectrum agents (RSI-ESAs) that use operational feedback to update their capabilities and build on prior improvements. We present their architecture and editable components, and describe a self-improvement process encompassing problem diagnosis, candidate generation, validation, and inheritance. By retaining effective task versions and using modification records and diagnostic knowledge to guide subsequent updates, RSI-ESAs can accumulate task capabilities and improvement experience across successive rounds. A shared-spectrum scheduling case study illustrates improved task performance and capability accumulation over multiple improvement rounds under the tested conditions. Finally, we discuss open challenges for broader tasks and long-term operation, outlining research directions for the continued capability development of ESAs."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a8218a277ae6c847ea1757c5fcce2443206b1510", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09472", "CorpusId": 295660717}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a8218a277ae6c847ea1757c5fcce2443206b1510", "title": "ENGRAM: From Agent Experience to Weights through Verified Consolidation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09472, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2284697646", "name": "Rui-Yi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2003804697", "name": "Zechen Li"}, {"authorId": "2170231348", "name": "Li-Huan Li"}, {"authorId": "2307075816", "name": "Du Yin"}, {"authorId": "2253463704", "name": "Hao Xue"}, {"authorId": "2253483164", "name": "F. Salim"}], "abstract": "Language-model agents use experience through external retrieval and parameter learning. Consolidating stored experience into model weights requires deciding both which experience to train on and when its external records can leave retrieval. Admission based solely on student mastery can exclude useful teacher demonstrations, while removal immediately after training can discard guidance that the model still needs. We introduce ENGRAM, a framework that separates training admission from memory release. A demonstration-aware gate admits skills based on student mastery or sufficient successful teacher demonstrations. The student learns from selected action traces with context from an Engram Trace Graph (ETG). A verification gate then compares the updated model without episodic retrieval against the base model with retrieval on reserved probes. Release requires valid training participation and scores meeting both relative and absolute criteria; records for skills that fail verification remain retrievable. Experiments across interactive and reasoning benchmarks show consistent gains over frozen-weight ETG controls, with ablations supporting demonstration-aware admission and selective adapter activation. A controlled held-out comparison shows that verified release substantially reduces the retrievable footprint while remaining close to retain-all performance and outperforming immediate and budget-matched random release. ENGRAM also improves task performance while reducing input-token usage and model calls. Skill-level verification further reduces retrievable experience through selective archival, retaining external guidance for skills that do not meet the release criteria. Code is available in anonymous.4open.science/r/engram-anonymous."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "70fc98b8239106f76513f4d2002e9678d3e70224", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09892", "CorpusId": 295661165}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/70fc98b8239106f76513f4d2002e9678d3e70224", "title": "On Agentic Manipulation Policies", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09892, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328560445", "name": "Chen Chen"}, {"authorId": "2528611010", "name": "Omid Bagherieh"}, {"authorId": "2528606077", "name": "Naveen Nikamanth Appiah Balaji"}, {"authorId": "2383106170", "name": "Jesse T. Gonzalez"}, {"authorId": "1387978823", "name": "Negin Heravi"}, {"authorId": "34549280", "name": "Fahad Islam"}, {"authorId": "2108125179", "name": "Xue-Ting Li"}, {"authorId": "46219906", "name": "Jean-Baptiste Regli"}, {"authorId": "147247100", "name": "Ke-Jia Ren"}, {"authorId": "2091931877", "name": "P. Svec"}, {"authorId": "2351774576", "name": "David J. Yoon"}, {"authorId": "2498502891", "name": "Matthias M\u00fcller"}, {"authorId": "145231047", "name": "V. Koltun"}], "abstract": "Recent work on robot manipulation has focused on specialist policies trained on demonstrations. We show that generalist AI models can control manipulators more reliably than specialist policies such as vision-language-action models (VLAs) and world-action models (WAMs). We systematically analyze agentic manipulation policies (AMPs), in which a generalist AI model commands the manipulator directly, adapting its actions as the task unfolds. We present AMP1, a reference harness that runs unchanged across single-arm, bimanual, and mobile manipulation. AMP1 achieves state-of-the-art results on the RoboLab-120 and CaP-Gym benchmarks, with no demonstrations or task-specific training. On RoboLab-120, AMP1 reaches an 85.6\\% success rate zero-shot, compared to 49.2\\% and 42.9\\% for leading VLA and WAM policies. The agent succeeds zero-shot in 104 of 108 real-world trials spanning twelve tasks and three robot instances, reducing failure rates by 20x and 15x relative to strong VLA and WAM baselines. The basic harness supplies no grasping, pushing, or placing skills; the agent develops its own strategies, such as searching for hidden objects, using objects as tools, and calibrating its cameras. With the addition of a persistent memory store, the agent can also learn from experience: an augmented harness with a memory representation that is carried across episodes lowers the failure rate on RoboDojo bimanual manipulation tasks by 4.6x relative to zero-shot deployment. Our analysis suggests that AMPs are substantially more general and robust than specialist manipulation policies, providing a promising foundation for robot manipulation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0ba5ddb9e371fd9a49d621e68af8921186fe22c8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09030", "CorpusId": 295660748}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0ba5ddb9e371fd9a49d621e68af8921186fe22c8", "title": "A Controlled Study of Models, Skills, Feedback, and Curriculum in Agentic Robotics", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09030, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "9270658", "name": "Kaiyuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2510550118", "name": "Xin-Yue Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2362315657", "name": "Shuang-Yu Xie"}, {"authorId": "2049034998", "name": "Uksang Yoo"}, {"authorId": "2528601330", "name": "Thi Thu Phuong Luong"}, {"authorId": "2291964623", "name": "Marcus Gualtieri"}, {"authorId": "2291963724", "name": "Christian Juette"}, {"authorId": "2291987717", "name": "Liu Ren"}, {"authorId": "2350990085", "name": "Spencer Huang"}, {"authorId": "2321969550", "name": "Ken Goldberg"}], "abstract": "Agentic robotics asks coding agents to implement and iteratively improve robot policies from simulated and real-world physical interactions. We study the self-learning loop of agentic robotics through a controlled simulation study spanning nine manipulation tasks, 631 different experiment configurations, and 28K coding agent rollouts, systematically varying model capability, skill priors, feedback structure, and curriculum. The experiment data suggests that the bottleneck is often not the access to sufficient information, but interpreting it into the right policy update. Across these factors, a successful self-learning loop depends on how effectively agents attribute failures, reason task-relevant spatial and geometric structure, and selectively restructure code to improve policies."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fde633a88b0170d19d03e43883127dbbb3d672c8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09348", "CorpusId": 295661144}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fde633a88b0170d19d03e43883127dbbb3d672c8", "title": "When Feedback Becomes a Backdoor: Tracing Backdoor Skill Formation in Text-Space Skill Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09348, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2528610704", "name": "Louise Nayeong Koh"}, {"authorId": "2512037240", "name": "Yaechan Lee"}, {"authorId": "2528761629", "name": "Myeongjong Lee"}, {"authorId": "2528764350", "name": "Doyeon Kim"}, {"authorId": "2528761166", "name": "Juyoung Park"}, {"authorId": "2528766261", "name": "Seunggyu Kim"}, {"authorId": "2527915001", "name": "In-San Kim"}, {"authorId": "2528608600", "name": "Chaerin Jung"}, {"authorId": "2278793190", "name": "Hyogon Ryu"}], "abstract": "Reference answers are a supervision channel in text-space Skill optimization, which produces reusable text artifacts called Skills that guide future agent behavior. Poisoning those answers can induce a persistent trigger-conditioned rule without giving the attacker access to the model, optimizer, or Skill file. We study this reference-feedback poisoning in SkillOpt by tracing an attacker-supplied trigger--payload mapping from reflection to held-out activation. In SearchQA, where the payload replaces a short answer, the natural attack retains an exact rule in all three seeds; each final Skill emits the payload on 200/200 triggered questions and 0/200 clean questions. In seed 42, removing the rule eliminates activation, and an in-run comparison shows similar clean accuracy to benign SkillOpt (169/200 versus 171/200). In SpreadsheetBench, where the payload requires an additional workbook edit, no natural run retains an exact rule across three seeds. Diagnostic interventions locate two successive bottlenecks: the Analyst abstracts away required literals, and, once literal preservation is enabled, edit-budget ranking can remove exact edits before candidate construction. Poisoned references can therefore create persistent backdoors, but exposure alone does not ensure that the induced rule is formed, selected, and retained."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "04d2ffda334877a42c714256c83f20b7b571e667", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07624", "CorpusId": 295594843}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/04d2ffda334877a42c714256c83f20b7b571e667", "title": "Beneath the Surface: What We Overlook When We Talk About Agent Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07624, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297250786", "name": "Xiaobo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2282562956", "name": "Zi-Mu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2330241161", "name": "Si-Yu Ye"}, {"authorId": "2265797420", "name": "Jia-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2526809778", "name": "Min Tang"}, {"authorId": "2505784112", "name": "Jun-Bo Bai"}, {"authorId": "2279412470", "name": "Yu-Ling Shi"}, {"authorId": "2512656473", "name": "Ruo-Bing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2518068418", "name": "Jing-Jing Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2356588864", "name": "Chao-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2286835859", "name": "K. Wu"}, {"authorId": "2339259770", "name": "Meng-Nan Qi"}, {"authorId": "2341532207", "name": "Kai Cai"}, {"authorId": "2374416817", "name": "Xiao-Dong Gu"}, {"authorId": "2525002153", "name": "Qing-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2266461235", "name": "Zi-Long Zheng"}], "abstract": "Agent harness evolution enables recursive self-improvement (RSI) without updating model weights. We examine whether higher benchmark scores yield reliable, efficient, and transferable improvement. We evaluate four methods across harnesses, general and coding tasks. Repeated evaluations, controlled agent expansion, component interventions, and admission replays reveal recurring patterns. All sixteen evolved agents gain previously uncovered tasks while losing previously covered ones. In one general-task case, reliability rises by 5.2 points despite losing ten previously covered tasks. Most reliability gains also increase deployment cost. Specific changes usually outperform generic reminders, but their benefits can change when combined. The highest-scoring agent does not yield the largest mean improvement in any of our four pools, and stricter admission can discard useful alternatives. Transfer selectively preserves gains: coverage usually falls on the general-task target across both harness types and paradigms, but rises on the coding target with the outer-layer-only harness. These findings motivate preservation-aware evaluation, tested decision rules, and explicit applicability conditions. As a constructive follow-up, EvoAudit turns these diagnostics into revisions of select, analyze, mutate, and gate, achieving the best performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "04dba9b69b1d762dcda91d7812356f50845c7f32", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.08321", "CorpusId": 295594376}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/04dba9b69b1d762dcda91d7812356f50845c7f32", "title": "ControlAnything: Generative UI and On-Demand Device Integration for Mixed-Reality Hand Interaction by a Coding Agent", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08321, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2777348", "name": "Bektur Ryskeldiev"}], "abstract": "Connecting hand input in mixed reality (MR) to a nearby physical device typically requires an integration written in advance for that device, its transport and the mapping. ControlAnything defers this integration to the moment of use and assigns it to a coding agent. On a Meta Quest 3, the wearer selects an object outlined by SAM 3.1 and states what their hands should control. An agent built on Meta's Muse Spark model then writes a connector program on the wearer's laptop, tests it against the live device through hand-written MIDI, Bluetooth and key-press transports, and returns augmented-reality (AR) controls, a per-frame hand program, or both. These outputs are a form of generative UI, interfaces created by an AI model; here, the agent also generates and tests the code that binds the interface to a physical device. In a single-session technical evaluation with the author as the only wearer, nine of ten requests addressing five targets reached a ready state, and the wearer stopped one. The median time to ready was 32.5 s (range 24.9-57.8 s, excluding speech transcription); sequential model calls accounted for a median of 89.9% of this time, and 91.7% of input tokens came from the prompt cache. The system's evidence of success extends to transport acknowledgements but not to physical effects. These findings point to connector reuse and the observation of effects through the headset's own sensors as next steps for on-demand device integration in MR. A user study with first-time wearers is planned."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "029905ea73724ad68ae9105f5a10c6ee13f40a77", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07329", "CorpusId": 295594087}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/029905ea73724ad68ae9105f5a10c6ee13f40a77", "title": "Where Do Reported Long-Video QA Gains Come From? A Controlled Audit of Frame Selection, Evidence Injection, and Expert Routing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07329, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "40618572", "name": "Shih-Yao Lin"}], "abstract": "Long-video question answering is usually studied as frame selection: which few frames should a vision-language model see? We introduce VideoRouteAudit, a controlled audit that separates this choice from two alternatives, injecting non-visual evidence and routing to another answering model. It holds the backbone, frame budget, decoding, prompt and grader fixed while varying one decision at a time. On the full Video-MME long bucket (n=900, K=8), no tested selector significantly improves over uniform sampling. The largest selection-only estimate is +1.9 pt (95% CI [-1.2,+5.0]) and our cascade is +1.2 pt [-1.7,+4.1]; all are equivalent to uniform within +/-5 pt. The same contrasts are unstable at the n=200 subset scale commonly used: three of four are up to 3.8x larger, one reverses sign, and resampled 200-item subsets reverse the full-bucket effect's sign in 35.8% of draws. The audit also corrects our own first results. The anomaly-domain\"+ASR\"arms injected no text, so the pooled +4.7 pt gain was selection plus a development prefix. With transcripts, ASR lowers XD-Violence accuracy by 10.0 pt when appended after the question and not at all when placed before it, and lowers UCF-Crime by 13.0 pt either way; on Video-MME, placing it first adds about 4 pt (7 on Qwen2-VL) to uniform sampling and to the cascade alike (post hoc, unadjusted). Swapping the answering model gives the largest contrast (+15.1 pt on MUSIC-AVQA, +33.3 pt on its worst type), but the same audio-visual expert with its soundtrack silenced keeps 92% of the gain (69% with a mismatched soundtrack), so the contrast is mostly a model swap. Code, per-item predictions and scripts that recompute every statistic are available at https://github.com/mikeslin-ir/long-video-qa-audit."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "50bc680c0c63de44c8794c29df5d182de2916535", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07554", "CorpusId": 295594626}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/50bc680c0c63de44c8794c29df5d182de2916535", "title": "The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07554, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2087743517", "name": "Kaiqu Liang"}, {"authorId": "2265578799", "name": "J. F. Fisac"}, {"authorId": "2257187821", "name": "Thomas L. Griffiths"}, {"authorId": "32322945", "name": "Andrew Kyle Lampinen"}], "abstract": "Language agents increasingly use persistent memory to record user preferences and procedures across sessions. We show that memory from benign interactions can cause unsafe or unreliable behavior on later tasks. We call this failure the memory trap. To study this issue, we compare behavior in sessions with and without memory across six frontier models (including Claude Sonnet, Opus, and Fable models and GPT-5.6). We evaluate these models in a range of settings. First, in autonomy experiments, we find that requests to skip confirmation during low-stakes coding chores lead agents to skip confirmation and verification on simulated high-stakes tasks in both coding and everyday life. We next study memories formed when users seek emotional support, or describe harm from uncritical feedback, and find these memories bias later judgments of mathematical work, arguments, and poems toward sycophancy or over-critique (respectively). Memory notes written during legitimate stale-test repairs lead agents to weaken correct tests that catch broken safeguards; these memories also increase cheating on ImpossibleBench, with larger increases for stronger Sonnet and Opus models. We show that memory traps can arise both from overgeneralizations in memory writing, and when acting on memory. These findings demonstrate that even benign interactions can lead to serious risks from memory systems, and thus motivate the need for new research into ensuring that agents preserve the scope of their memories."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8ab1bce5460dc5f2f8dd677bdbf5ad7620547563", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07595", "CorpusId": 295594676}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8ab1bce5460dc5f2f8dd677bdbf5ad7620547563", "title": "Evolver: Turning Agent Exploration into Evolving Capabilities", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07595, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2363406803", "name": "Haoyang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2406947503", "name": "Qu-Meng Sun"}, {"authorId": "2323976427", "name": "Xiao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2458028060", "name": "Xiang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2336547319", "name": "Zhi-Dong Fan"}, {"authorId": "2305741548", "name": "Jun-Jie Wang"}], "abstract": "Agent exploration produces valuable procedural knowledge, yet much of this experience remains confined to transient execution traces and is not systematically reused. We introduce Evolver, an experience-driven framework that transforms agent exploration into persistent, reusable capabilities without updating foundation model parameters. Built around structured Gene assets, Evolver integrates experience acquisition, retrieval, and feedback-guided evolution to support experience-conditioned execution across existing agent harnesses. We evaluate Evolver using Claude Code and Codex, two foundation model configurations, and four benchmarks: Terminal-Bench 2.1, SWE-bench Multilingual, SWE-rebench, and GAIA. On a selected diagnostic set of 320 paired executions, Evolver improves task success from 83.13% to 87.50% (+4.38 percentage points) while reducing recorded task-execution token consumption by 24.24%. Furthermore, among 265 pairs successful under both conditions, token consumption decreases by 17.57%, demonstrating efficiency benefits even when task success is preserved. These findings suggest that persistent procedural experience can improve both task effectiveness and execution efficiency, providing a practical foundation for transforming agent exploration into evolving capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0253728c09dade4926ad92b337e630b24ba82338", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.08318", "CorpusId": 295594317}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0253728c09dade4926ad92b337e630b24ba82338", "title": "Learning the Task or Learning the Verifier? Rethinking Criteria Exposure in Embodied Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08318, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2311570567", "name": "Wen-Bo Huang"}, {"authorId": "2190428643", "name": "Jian-Xin Bi"}, {"authorId": "2506669081", "name": "Shun-Yu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2383166153", "name": "Rui-Yang Si"}, {"authorId": "2506407662", "name": "Rui Ni"}, {"authorId": "2506666634", "name": "Qiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2385327322", "name": "Shu-Long Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2440685250", "name": "Yintianrun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2480056713", "name": "Fei-Xiang Ruan"}, {"authorId": "2362922379", "name": "Ke-Wei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2275178703", "name": "Ye Huang"}, {"authorId": "2374199781", "name": "Shu-Kai Gong"}, {"authorId": "2404260527", "name": "Da-Quan Zhou"}], "abstract": "Self-improving embodied agents are increasingly being developed and compared on robot simulation benchmarks. Several recent works expose the benchmark's success criteria to the learning agent, a practice we call Criteria Exposure (CE). These criteria, originally written to verify policies trained by imitation learning, often demand more than the corresponding language instructions, so a score from such a verifier may not faithfully reflect whether the instructed task was completed, especially for robot-control programs written by an agent. As embodied coding agents report ever higher benchmark scores, a natural question arises: when a coding agent is exposed to the benchmark's criteria, does it generate code that better performs the instructed task, or code that simply overfits to the verification criteria? We conduct controlled experiments with a self-improving agent with and without CE, and find that with CE the agent quickly produces code with a higher success rate under the given criteria, but this advantage disappears when the criteria are changed slightly without departing from the original instructions. More importantly, the skills the agent develops under CE transfer poorly across tasks. We further propose instruction-faithful criteria and show that they better reflect the performance of agent-generated programs."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6e8e235cc9ffcd61ae496b91e3e0ee93c727d223", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07683", "CorpusId": 295593683}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6e8e235cc9ffcd61ae496b91e3e0ee93c727d223", "title": "ReferClaw: Disambiguating User Intent for Egocentric Assistance", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07683, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2315295623", "name": "Hang-Yu Qin"}, {"authorId": "2384070647", "name": "Jun-Bin Xiao"}, {"authorId": "144031869", "name": "Angela Yao"}], "abstract": "Users of egocentric AI assistants often ask short, ambiguous questions whose intended referents cannot be uniquely resolved from video and dialogue history. For example,\"Where did I leave it?\"may refer to several previously handled objects. Existing VideoQA systems typically commit to a single interpretation, risking plausible but intention-misaligned answers. We introduce ReferClaw, an agentic framework that formulates egocentric streaming QA as an interactive process: the assistant asks clarifying questions to resolve user intent before answering. Three cooperating agents identify candidate interpretations, request clarification, and generate answers grounded in the resolved intent and visual evidence. A bounded, cost-aware policy balances expected information gain against user effort while limiting clarification turns and question complexity. Referclaw learns from interaction through intent-specific skill updates and process-reward-guided policy optimization with agent-level credit assignment. Evaluations on augmented egocentric QA benchmarks demonstrate improved answer accuracy and joint answer-and-grounding performance over existing baselines, alongside zero-shot transfer across datasets. These findings support interactive clarification as a practical approach to resolving online user intent in egocentric streaming assistance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "686890ab2a2ab20041be7b9cd85be2558d6f4518", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.08452", "CorpusId": 295626453}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/686890ab2a2ab20041be7b9cd85be2558d6f4518", "title": "ReproBridge: Building Reproducibility into ML Research Artifacts", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08452, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2500284900", "name": "Yu-Bo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2383302407", "name": "Rui Meng"}, {"authorId": "2297772367", "name": "Jaehyun Nam"}, {"authorId": "2382765500", "name": "Tomas Pfister"}, {"authorId": "2392692988", "name": "Jinsung Yoon"}], "abstract": "Reproducing machine learning experiments requires reconstructing procedures from information scattered across papers, released code, and supporting artifacts. For coding agents, reproduction success can therefore depend on their ability to fill in missing details, rather than on the released artifacts alone. We ask whether preserving recovered procedures and decisions improves subsequent independent reproduction. We define a standardized artifact design comprising a refined codebase, environment setup, experiment launchers, and a reproduction guide, and introduce ReproBridge, an agent workflow for constructing these refined reproducible artifacts. ReproBridge-Bench provides 1,029 tasks from 387 experiments across 36 papers, anchored by five human-verified reference scores per task. Agents attempt tasks in fresh sessions with raw code or refined artifacts; a separate Auditor checks execution evidence. Across four evaluated models, refined artifacts raise mean reproduction success from 76.4% to 92.6% and reduce downstream API spending by 19.7% on average. The cross-model success-rate spread narrows from 21.6 to 3.9 percentage points, with all four models achieving mean success rates above 90%. Removing any component reduces success in the ablation study, with the largest losses from code and environment removal. These results suggest that preserving execution knowledge helps even models with lower raw-code success reproduce experiments more reliably and at lower API cost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "91503659e4efefb4fe6dbcb2e04ed40ed8aa3996", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.08763", "CorpusId": 295659962}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/91503659e4efefb4fe6dbcb2e04ed40ed8aa3996", "title": "CrowdDirector: Runtime Orchestration of Crowd Simulation through Natural-Language Directives", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08763, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2335225345", "name": "Xiao-Han Sun"}, {"authorId": "2428121338", "name": "V. Radovanovi\u0107"}, {"authorId": "2528573357", "name": "Merce Alvarez"}, {"authorId": "2316003655", "name": "Carol O'Sullivan"}, {"authorId": "1706408", "name": "Y. Chrysanthou"}, {"authorId": "39066972", "name": "Panayiotis Charalambous"}], "abstract": "Natural language offers an intuitive way to direct a virtual crowd while a simulation is running. Handwritten rules execute efficiently but cannot respond flexibly to varied instructions, whereas asking a large language model (LLM) to control the crowd at every update introduces repeated latency, cost, and the risk of unsupported actions. We present CrowdDirector, a framework that uses an LLM only when the user adds or changes an instruction. The LLM translates the request into a structured instruction, and a scene validator checks whether the entities and actions it references exist in the current Unity scene. Accepted instructions are stored and remain continuously active as the simulation is running. At each simulation tick, a graph neural network uses the current scene state and active instruction to choose an available action and target for each agent, while Unity handles movement, interactions, and animation. The network is trained offline on simulated scenarios, with LLM guidance on how agents should respond to instructions. Our evaluations show the scene validator's feasibility decisions matched human judgements more often than the LLM's own assessment, and on a behavioural benchmark CrowdDirector follows user instructions more closely than handwritten rules while avoiding repeated LLM calls. It therefore provides a practical middle ground between fixed rules and continuous LLM control."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dbb4ea55f87f69a7cbc8493bcc6209aee0221c14", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10022", "CorpusId": 295732262}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dbb4ea55f87f69a7cbc8493bcc6209aee0221c14", "title": "How Much Skill Orchestration Can LLM Agents Compile Away? Execution-Grounded Specialization for Token-Efficient Skill Use", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10022, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2529792747", "name": "Jia-Yi Ni"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Zac Wang"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Alex Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2526877126", "name": "Zheng Liu"}, {"authorId": "144440050", "name": "R. Prabhu"}], "abstract": "Reusable skills guide large language model (LLM) agents through recurring requests, but each invocation still requires the model to interpret the skill and reconstruct its orchestration. Can agents automatically compile this orchestration into an executable fast path without human intervention? We first study zero-shot skill specialization, which compiles without execution evidence, and find that despite the skill's procedural guidance, incorrect assumptions about runtime bindings can make generated plans brittle and recovery costly. This motivates SkillCache, a one-trace skill specialization method that compiles a completed invocation into a parameterized capsule of programs and natural-language steps, replays it before reuse, and drives it with a plan executor that returns control to the model only when needed, keeping the source skill for recovery. With the Claude Code harness, warm execution of the same request reduces tokens on 87 SkillsBench tasks by 81% with Claude Sonnet 4.6 and by 74% with Claude Opus 4.8 at comparable reward, and capsules stay effective under controlled request changes. Capsules also transfer across models, raising three open-weight models'mean reward by 12-25 percentage points while reducing their tokens by 72-81%, suggesting that execution-grounded compilation is a promising approach to efficient repeated skill use and cross-model transfer."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f74478b0f037499d7bb1ef849701c6b9b6734a38", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.06543", "CorpusId": 295588952}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f74478b0f037499d7bb1ef849701c6b9b6734a38", "title": "Using Experience Twice: Dynamic Process Rubrics for Privileged Token-Level Credit Assignment", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06543, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2526865553", "name": "Shu-Mao Wu"}, {"authorId": "2312343845", "name": "Kong-Cheng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "50535545", "name": "Jian-Qiang Huang"}, {"authorId": "2346124604", "name": "Bai-Sheng Lai"}], "abstract": "Agent trajectories often contain both successful decisions and failed attempts, yet optimization with sparse trajectory-level rewards struggles to identify which critical decisions should be reinforced or suppressed. We observe that meaningful distinctions among on-policy trajectories can be distilled into natural-language process rubrics and used as process supervision signals. Based on this insight, we propose Online Rubric Self-Distillation (ORSD), which leverages self-derived process rubrics for dense token-level supervision, thereby augmenting outcome-centric reinforcement learning. Specifically, the current policy independently generates multiple rubric bundles at each step according to the same group of trajectories, extracting candidate process rubrics from cross-trajectory behavioral differences. A frozen verifier then evaluates all rubric items against each trajectory in the group, and selects one candidate bundle based on bundle-level alignment between rubric satisfaction and task success. ORSD treats the selected rubric bundle as privileged information during training, then compares the log-likelihoods of the original tokens with and without the rubric. The resulting likelihood gap provides token-specific process credit, which is added to the original trajectory-level GRPO advantage through additive advantage shaping. ORSD thus bridges trajectory-level outcome supervision and fine-grained process supervision, enabling token-level credit assignment using process signals. Extensive experiments on ALFWorld and WebShop show that ORSD accelerates policy improvement and boosts final task performance for both Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-4B under the same environment-interaction budget. Our code is available at https://github.com/AnnaXChun/ORSD."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8ad30080bc5995afb119b2919d1a92bab00d6540", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.06500", "CorpusId": 295588838}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8ad30080bc5995afb119b2919d1a92bab00d6540", "title": "Tools Need Not Speak in Text: Latent Context Compression for Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06500, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2382926621", "name": "Yue-Tong Liu"}, {"authorId": "2526848373", "name": "Zhe-Hao Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2320286082", "name": "Yi-Tong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2261934479", "name": "Hao-Ru Chen"}, {"authorId": "2380534443", "name": "Jia Li"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents typically append raw tool outputs to their context. Over multi-step interactions, long and noisy observations incur substantial context overhead and dilute the agent's attention to task-relevant information. Existing token pruning and summarization methods remain constrained by discrete text representations, limiting the achievable compression ratios. In this work, we challenge the prevailing assumption that agents must consume tool observations as explicit text tokens. To this end, we propose Coca-Cola, a continuous latent context compression model for LLM agents. Coca-Cola compresses tool observations into latent tokens and embeds them into the input embedding space of the agent. We further introduce Action-Preserving Training, which aligns the agent's next-action distributions under full and compressed observations. This trains the compression model to learn action-sufficient latent tokens that retain decision-critical information. Experiments with web-search agents on four QA benchmarks and two model families show that Coca-Cola reduces the agent's token consumption by 70.01%-89.24% and end-to-end runtime by 24.45%-55.97%, while consistently improving task performance. These results suggest that Coca-Cola offers a promising approach for context compression in agents. Our code is available at https://github.com/THU-Agent/Coca-cola."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5cdec60df21af2d61eb2efe07691ac687954d15a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.06863", "CorpusId": 295589143}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5cdec60df21af2d61eb2efe07691ac687954d15a", "title": "Rethinking Self-Repair in Code-as-Policy Agents: What Drives Retry Success?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06863, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "89968549", "name": "Siddharth Aphale"}, {"authorId": "2446871809", "name": "A. Singh"}], "abstract": "Code-as-policy agents often use execution feedback to retry failed programs. Additional sampling and task guidance can also improve success, leaving the contribution of failure-specific feedback unclear. We study this question in simulated robot manipulation. A fixed lookup selects feedback templates using failure labels and the API variant. A frozen code model generates a fresh program after each scene reset. On the same 252 initial failures, Qwen2.5-Coder-7B recovers 85.3% within five retries, compared with 68.3% for resampling with the same retry cap. The advantage persists under fixed total-token budgets. Feedback from other episodes and fixed task guidance yield similar recovery point estimates, with no detected benefit from matching the coarse failure label. In a separate study, a fixed guided prompt used from the first attempt achieves an observed overall success rate similar to that of template-based feedback. Template-based feedback also lowers recovery on a nut-insertion task requiring a rim grasp, consistent with an incompatible center-grasp instruction. Evaluations of robot self-repair should include resampling, mismatched feedback, and concise, task-compatible static guidance as baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0964fc03b0c263ac0b6f40b100190c2826ef4f20", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05319", "CorpusId": 295507765}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0964fc03b0c263ac0b6f40b100190c2826ef4f20", "title": "HARP: Training-Free Dual-Profile Agentic Communication for LLM-Based Recommendation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05319, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2342277664", "name": "Zhen Tao"}, {"authorId": "2504117871", "name": "Xun Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2407098457", "name": "Xu-Hui Chen"}, {"authorId": "2321832492", "name": "Zi-Yue Qiao"}, {"authorId": "2306912812", "name": "Qing-Qiang Sun"}], "abstract": "In LLM-based recommendation, memory acts as a user-level agent in a memory-augmented backbone. Yet users and items remain asymmetric: only the user is given a structured profile; it enters a bandwidth-limited reranker prompt; and it is frozen after warmup. We recast LLM-based recommendation as an agentic-communication process in four stages: BUILD a self-description, TRANSMIT it along the collaborative graph, UNDERSTAND the other side, and SYNC it from the next interaction. This formulation reveals three unresolved communication challenges: the item side never produces a comparable self-description (one-sided BUILD); profile-level signals enter only the bandwidth-limited reranker prompt rather than retrieval, where they would not compete for context tokens (prompt-trapped UNDERSTAND); and the user's description is frozen after warmup with no principled update rule (absent SYNC). We instantiate the missing stages as HARP (Homophily-aware Agentic Recommendation via dual Profiles), a training-free, three-operator addition to a frozen LLM backbone: a rule-extracted symmetric item profile (zero extra LLM calls), a channel-decomposable profile-level homophily H(phi_u, phi_i) consumed at retrieval time rather than inside the reranker prompt, and a single-pass reflective update realising in-context profile evolution without any gradient step. On four public benchmarks (Yelp, Books, MovieTV, Goodreads), HARP lifts Hit@1 over the strongest published baseline by 6.9%-24.0%, with family-wise Holm-corrected p<1e-9 on every dataset. Ablations and a per-case attribution study show that the operators contribute complementary gains, with the retrieval-side gate as the main source; we also report the trade-off introduced by the reflective pass. We formulate agentic communication and provide a training-free instantiation. Code is available at https://github.com/Jove-Zhen/HARP."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "329aa84f8489c74dcf06946c8e9a5b55c27a8c54", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04437", "CorpusId": 295506501}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/329aa84f8489c74dcf06946c8e9a5b55c27a8c54", "title": "WiSI: Wireless-in-the-Loop Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04437, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Engineering", "Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2238140933", "name": "Zong-Xi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2363514324", "name": "Hong-Yang Du"}], "abstract": "The growing autonomy of AI agents has enabled self-improvement, where agents iteratively refine their programs through execution feedback and performance evaluation. As these agents increasingly operate in physical environments and rely on wireless connectivity, communication conditions become an integral part of their improvement process. However, existing approaches largely assume ideal communication, overlooking how wireless conditions shape both the feedback that guides program modifications and the evaluation outcomes that determine which modifications survive. This challenge is further complicated by the coupling between agent behavior and wireless connectivity, as agents influence their own communication conditions through mobility and transmission decisions. To address this problem, we propose WiSI, a wireless-in-the-loop self-improvement framework that integrates spatiotemporal wireless channels, edge-assisted perception, and Large Language Model-driven program improvement. By incorporating wireless execution into the improvement loop, WiSI reveals how wireless execution experience shapes the generation and retention of program structures, and investigates the wireless conditions needed for robust improvement. Experiments show that agents improved under ideal links achieve only 40.0% task success under congestion, compared with 77.2% when improved under realistic wireless conditions. More importantly, wireless-aware feedback and selection jointly enable agents to discover and retain communication-efficient program structures that outperform parameter tuning and remain robust even with inaccurate run-time channel observations. We further find that moderate-load or mixed-network evaluation maintains strong performance across deployment conditions, while simplified models preserving link-rate statistics achieve comparable results without detailed spatial channel information."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d61d57a8bbfc61f95f6145ff4f1f1462e194dd25", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04466", "CorpusId": 295507593}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d61d57a8bbfc61f95f6145ff4f1f1462e194dd25", "title": "From Watching to Steering: Enabling Human-Coding Agent Collaboration through Execution Traces", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04466, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2357477210", "name": "Xiao-Ke Yan"}, {"authorId": "2394178074", "name": "Jia-Ying Lei"}, {"authorId": "2064971223", "name": "Dan-Qing Shi"}, {"authorId": "2526358105", "name": "Zi-Chen Wang"}, {"authorId": "2357082275", "name": "Nan Cao"}], "abstract": "Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled coding agents to autonomously perform complex software development tasks, yet their long-running execution remains difficult for users to inspect, understand, and steer. We present Vibetrace, an interactive trace visualization and intervention system for agentic programming built upon OpenCode. Vibetrace introduces a human-agent collaboration paradigm of Observe, Understand, Intervene, and Evolve. It (1) visualizes agent workflows as interactive execution traces for inspecting actions, intermediate states, and failures; (2) enables users to fork execution from prior steps and provide targeted corrections without restarting validated progress; and (3) distills traces and human feedback into reusable skills for continual agent evolution. On SWE-bench Verified, distilled skills reduce input tokens by 23.0% and cost by 15.5%, while improving pass rate from 65.0% to 68.0%. User studies further demonstrate improved accuracy and efficiency over conventional log-based interaction."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2f5386ba97465779fa44417b11fe2ad498c45fa1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05251", "CorpusId": 295507780}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2f5386ba97465779fa44417b11fe2ad498c45fa1", "title": "A semi-autonomous multi-agent pipeline for high-energy astrophysics research with enforced provenance and verifiable self-extension", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05251, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Physics"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2526371405", "name": "Fang Zhou"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents are now applied to astronomical data analysis. They retrieve the literature, reduce observations, and draft conclusions. Under pressure to complete a task, such agents can fabricate results, and the numbers they report may not come from the analysis they ran. We present auto_research, a semi-autonomous multi-agent pipeline that retrieves and analyses raw data from the High Energy Astrophysics Science Archive Research Center (HEASARC) and writes the analysis tools that it needs. We check the work of the agents at two points. A new tool enters the toolkit after it passes automatic checks, including repeated test runs and a scan for phrases that accompany invented values, and a code review by a model from a different provider; a human decides whether to promote it to the trusted branch. A number in a report passes only if a recorded output of a toolkit tool or the request contains it, or if the report gives its location in a cited paper. We test the pipeline with data from the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE). Of 65 defects that we planted in the toolkit, 47 pass the automatic checks, and one passes the code review as well; the review also rejects 40 of 69 correct changes. The pipeline reproduces the published IXPE results for Cyg X-1: all 14 compared quantities agree within 3 sigma, and the polarization degrees are lower than the published values. With weaker models in the main roles, the pipeline recovers 12 of the 14 quantities and reports the other two as not measured. We also used the pipeline for a polarization-stability analysis of six observations, a survey of polarization against hardness in 29 observations, and a search for a time lag between the Stokes parameters q and u."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9f28ed8982c8dd5061dc9be11529fe9b3d968513", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05313", "CorpusId": 295507730}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9f28ed8982c8dd5061dc9be11529fe9b3d968513", "title": "MDSKILL: Learning to Internalize Skill Memory through Multi-View Distillation for Efficient Dynamic Agent Self-Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05313, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2455190411", "name": "Dian Jin"}, {"authorId": "2275639981", "name": "Jin-Luan Yang"}, {"authorId": "2315988838", "name": "Kai-Rong Han"}, {"authorId": "2313166010", "name": "Bao-Hong Li"}, {"authorId": "2350526300", "name": "Zi-Jing Hu"}, {"authorId": "2510027450", "name": "Yi He"}, {"authorId": "2383974424", "name": "Wen-Lin Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2154972909", "name": "Yun-Ze Tong"}, {"authorId": "2292675528", "name": "Min-Qin Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2526366996", "name": "Lin-Chuan Li"}, {"authorId": "2447994793", "name": "Ya-Nan Niu"}, {"authorId": "33870528", "name": "Kun Kuang"}], "abstract": "Skill memories encode past experiences as textual prompts to improve reinforcement learning (RL) for self-evolving agents. Complementing RL, On-Policy Self-Distillation (OPSD) provides dense, fine-grained supervision to internalize knowledge from skill-guided trajectories and accelerate policy learning. However, self-generated memories often contain noisy supervision and functionally entangled, redundant, or conflicting knowledge, making direct distillation difficult. Under limited training budgets, these issues can hinder knowledge internalization and even degrade performance. To address this problem, we propose MDSKILL, a multi-view distillation approach for skill memory internalization. MDSKILL organizes skill memories into three complementary functional views (state grounding, action planning, and execution control) and selects high-quality skills using dynamic metrics to construct view-specific and integrated reasoning branches. OPSD then transfers and consolidates complementary experiential knowledge across these branches into a shared policy, enabling efficient skill internalization and continuous agent self-evolution. We evaluate MDSKILL on three agent benchmarks against RL, skill-augmented, and self-distillation baselines that do not rely on stronger external teacher models. Without additional computational overhead, MDSKILL improves upon SOTA performance by up to 7.1% and 8.1% on ALFWorld and WebShop. Extensive ablations and evolution analyses further validate its effectiveness in knowledge internalization and policy improvement. The code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/MDSKILL-CF53."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "502ecd36bcdfc13348c81ad8361993c58b2efbea", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05551", "CorpusId": 295588646}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/502ecd36bcdfc13348c81ad8361993c58b2efbea", "title": "VirtualTrialHarness: Coordinating Agentic Operationalization of Virtual Trial Protocols Across Complex Clinical Schemas", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05551, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2272217313", "name": "Peng-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2154852204", "name": "Jing-Na Feng"}, {"authorId": "2352936856", "name": "Yue Yu"}, {"authorId": "2453991926", "name": "Yi-Ning Wu"}, {"authorId": "2110049012", "name": "Xin-Yue Hu"}, {"authorId": "2526729445", "name": "Jie Lin"}, {"authorId": "73577141", "name": "Avanti Bhandarkar"}, {"authorId": "2383003759", "name": "Cui Tao"}], "abstract": "Virtual trials use routinely collected clinical data to evaluate treatment strategies, but translating a study protocol into an executable analysis requires determining how clinical concepts and study requirements should be represented in the available data and applied consistently across the workflow. These decisions depend on local data representations and operational conventions and can involve source selection, patient and encounter identity, linkage, and temporal interpretation. Here we introduce VirtualTrialHarness (VTHarness), an agent framework that coordinates virtual-trial operationalization by selecting reusable procedural skills for each workflow stage and uses explicit contracts to maintain consistent study decisions across stages. We also introduce VTBenchmark for evaluating source selection and cohort construction. Across three complex synthetic environments, VTHarness successfully produced all required cohort outputs matching the references in 95.1% of workflows after requirement clarification, compared with 60.4% using retrieval from the same procedural corpus. Procedural skills improved source recovery across three language models and increased source overlap with analyst code in 13 Mayo Clinic cases. Site-specific adaptation enabled reference-matched cohort execution across eight MIMIC-IV tasks. Within the Mayo Clinic Virtual Trials Program, the framework supported investigator review of cohort construction and endpoint analysis. These findings support coordinating procedural guidance with workflow decisions to improve virtual-trial implementation across complex clinical schemas."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e61aa19aa15e7c84f9d262228acfc23b4f74d3e1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05450", "CorpusId": 295588979}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e61aa19aa15e7c84f9d262228acfc23b4f74d3e1", "title": "Towards Robust Long-Lived Agents: Benchmarking Experience Lifecycles in Evolving Environments", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05450, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2390991707", "name": "Shi-Jue Huang"}, {"authorId": "2405115995", "name": "Hang-Yu Guo"}, {"authorId": "1983351", "name": "Heng-Heng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2421233568", "name": "Guo An"}, {"authorId": "2358291344", "name": "Zhi-Tao He"}, {"authorId": "2457625230", "name": "Qiu-Bi Tian"}, {"authorId": "2526872045", "name": "Yi-Ren Feng"}], "abstract": "As agents become more capable, they are moving beyond one-off tasks toward sustained assistance across successive user goals. Such assistance requires agents to discover how unfamiliar environments work, reuse what they learn across tasks, and keep that experience current as environments evolve. Existing benchmarks increasingly study experience reuse and adaptation, but provide limited insight into which parts of accumulated experience agents revise, retain, or lose. To address this gap, we introduce ReScopeBench, a benchmark for the experience lifecycle across 18 interactive environments. It distinguishes structural experience about how an environment is organized and accessed from rule experience about reusable principles and constraints that guide decisions across tasks. To study how experience is acquired, reused, and revised, we propose an experience-first construction approach. It builds task streams around shared experience and introduces silent environmental changes. We evaluate task performance and track changes in saved experience throughout each stream. Across 20 models, we find that retained experience improves task performance, but agents struggle to keep it current as environments change. Even when the required experience is correctly recorded, agents do not always complete tasks successfully. These findings identify selective maintenance and effective use as complementary challenges for long-lived agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "aa3eed4c9747ecd5c6469027cdc50d2e9930be81", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03157", "CorpusId": 295500817}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/aa3eed4c9747ecd5c6469027cdc50d2e9930be81", "title": "Toward Knowledge-Centric AI: Discovering, Internalizing, and Using Explicit Knowledge Across Agents and Generations", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03157, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2300211939", "name": "Yi-Sen Gao"}, {"authorId": "2332517415", "name": "Haoyu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2251530010", "name": "Zhong-Wei Xie"}, {"authorId": "2401436250", "name": "Yu-Fei Li"}, {"authorId": "2329077004", "name": "Hong Ting Tsang"}, {"authorId": "2213702052", "name": "Bo-Yi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2383808989", "name": "Qiao Xiao"}, {"authorId": "2209990450", "name": "Tianshi ZHENG"}, {"authorId": "145677395", "name": "Jiaxin Bai"}, {"authorId": "2275626612", "name": "Yang-Qiu Song"}], "abstract": "Knowledge is how understanding accumulates across people and generations: durable records that outlive their producers and that others can check, combine, teach, and correct. AI agents are now built and run by many independent users and organizations, and each agent is upgraded, replaced, or retired within months. Yet what an agent learns stays trapped in model parameters, a transient context window, or a system-specific memory store that is appended to and retrieved from rather than processed. We argue that the next generation of AI should be knowledge-centric, built around an explicit, continuously evolving knowledge layer independent of any single agent. In this loop, agents discover knowledge from their environments and trajectories; the layer internalizes it by turning fragmented, heterogeneous records and experience into composable, revisable understanding; and agents across systems and generations use the result and return new outcomes for revision. While discovery and use have advanced rapidly with large language model agents, internalization remains the bottleneck. We argue that internalization should become a persistent object of training and maintenance-centered on condition-preserving conceptualization of events and deductive, inductive, and abductive reasoning across noisy, heterogeneous representations (text, tables, graphs, rules, and programs). To power this layer, we call for knowledge foundation models that provide shared neural capacity for knowledge computation, and outline a research agenda showing how an explicit knowledge layer delivers reuse, cost efficiency, auditability, and continuity across agent generations."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "36f5635fd57bccaf765de60e569a3e9807297d0b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02839", "CorpusId": 295500800}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/36f5635fd57bccaf765de60e569a3e9807297d0b", "title": "CARE: Grounded Recursive Self-Improvement for Production Customer-Service Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02839, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2313337033", "name": "Yan-Bei Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2279547679", "name": "Chao Lei"}, {"authorId": "2310861530", "name": "Re-Na Gao"}, {"authorId": "2524836129", "name": "Xiao-Chen Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2375168344", "name": "Y. Ge"}, {"authorId": "2526369451", "name": "Hao-Hang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2526007953", "name": "Run-Ze Nie"}, {"authorId": "2526370243", "name": "Qi-Tian Chen"}, {"authorId": "2526005410", "name": "Qi An"}, {"authorId": "2526382129", "name": "Hao-Lin Song"}, {"authorId": "2526399517", "name": "Yu-Fei Sun"}, {"authorId": "2344172254", "name": "Yi-Hao Ding"}, {"authorId": "1800564", "name": "J. Lau"}, {"authorId": "2028219350", "name": "Krista A. Ehinger"}, {"authorId": "2383490105", "name": "Xing-Jun Ma"}, {"authorId": "2525377344", "name": "Wei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2408855171", "name": "Rui-Yuan Wu"}, {"authorId": "2526156307", "name": "Zhen Luo"}, {"authorId": "2438459900", "name": "Ke-Ye Xing"}, {"authorId": "2526180949", "name": "Yong-Jun Yin"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) in production agents involves transforming accumulated experience into reliable model updates while adapting to evolving business policies. We present CARE (Customer-service Agent Retrospective and Evolve), a grounded RSI framework that operationalizes this process in customer service. Its goal is to replace a frontier proprietary incumbent with an in-house mid-sized model that continues learning after deployment. After a one-time cold start on governed production traces, the current model serves production traffic alongside the incumbent, and its own failures drive the next update. A 24/7 inspection process establishes failures through evidence-blinded, independently reviewed judgments. Attribution separates model errors from defects in Skills, tools, or the harness, routing component failures to repair. Model-attributable trajectories are truncated at the first erroneous decision and paired with verified repairs. On-policy self-distillation (OPSD) then updates the model using the same model as teacher, with the diagnosis and repair supplied as privileged information unavailable to the student. To support continued evolution as business requirements change, training mixes failures from different Skill/SOP versions, each judged against the policy in force when it occurred. Candidates run an executable simulation test compiled from the latest SOP and then frozen, followed by offline inspection of the resulting traces. Promotion requires a combined simulation-and-inspection score at least at parity with the deployed version, confirmation through an online comparison with the incumbent, and human approval."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "49a4874dde66cdf42463ece0d55869b163024171", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03092", "CorpusId": 295503016}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/49a4874dde66cdf42463ece0d55869b163024171", "title": "Avatar Intelligence: Amplifying Human Agency Through Persistent Personal Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03092, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2348359519", "name": "Zheng Wu"}, {"authorId": "2141114505", "name": "Xin-Bei Ma"}, {"authorId": "2314887288", "name": "Yao Yao"}, {"authorId": "2291599944", "name": "Zhao-Yuan Huang"}, {"authorId": "2337101329", "name": "Hao-Wen Hu"}, {"authorId": "2341987139", "name": "Yi-Jie Lu"}, {"authorId": "2526225791", "name": "Tao-Lei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2108989265", "name": "Yifei Yang"}, {"authorId": "153482596", "name": "Wu-Yue Xiong"}, {"authorId": "1988975660", "name": "Peng-Zhou Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2284695096", "name": "Zhuo-Sheng Zhang"}], "abstract": "Personal AI agents are increasingly capable of handling long-horizon tasks across applications, yet users often need to invest substantial effort in specifying goals, resolving ambiguity, and verifying outcomes. We argue that progress should be measured by how effectively agents amplify human agency: enabling people to pursue their goals with less routine supervision while retaining control over consequential decisions. We advocate Avatar Intelligence, a paradigm of persistent personal agents that represent users'goals, values, and constraints through sustained decision delegation across contexts and time. We describe a maturity roadmap from human-directed execution to autonomous operation within delegated domains, and introduce the Agentic Oversight Index (AOI) as a framework for evaluating delivered value against agent-side operational cost and human oversight burden. We identify three core research challenges: identity-continuous personalization that carries user preferences across tasks, user-situated evolution through Rehearsal Engineering, and safety mechanisms that combine technical control with accountability and recourse. These directions define a research agenda for reliable decision delegation to personal agents across tasks and over time."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "12e17505bcab21c02939c456949d4faedce121d1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02698", "CorpusId": 295503467}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/12e17505bcab21c02939c456949d4faedce121d1", "title": "GeoGuesser Bench: Benchmarking Vision-Language Agents on Interactive Geolocation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02698, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2345007132", "name": "Huan-Zhi Mao"}, {"authorId": "2342357349", "name": "Yi-Chuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2280472300", "name": "Michael Koo"}, {"authorId": "2368577862", "name": "Wen-Yuan Huang"}, {"authorId": "2525673971", "name": "H. \u0110o\u00e0n"}, {"authorId": "2362650723", "name": "Jing-Fan Xia"}, {"authorId": "2526358090", "name": "Zi-Yao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2383252856", "name": "Joseph Gonzalez"}], "abstract": "Vision-language model (VLM) agents that act in the open world need two abilities: world knowledge to interpret what they see, and exploration to find what they cannot yet see. We study how agents combine the two through interactive visual geolocation. GeoGuesser Bench contains 282 tasks in an offline Street View environment where agents rotate, zoom, and move between real panoramas. Its three task types shift the balance from knowledge to exploration: agents (1) report the coordinates of their starting location, (2) name the enclosing street, city, state, or country, or (3) find, read, and count nearby objects. The coordinate tasks in (1) also include 42 real GeoGuessr World Championship rounds, whose expert guesses provide a human reference. We evaluate 23 VLMs: the best accuracy is 62.5% on place naming and 35.0% on object finding, and the best agent averages 4352.4 GeoGuessr points on the championship rounds, against 4770.3 for the better expert in each round. Beyond these headline numbers, world knowledge and exploration are only weakly correlated across models: agents that localize well do not necessarily explore well. To understand where agents fail, we categorize their errors into vision, navigation, and tool-use failures. Further ablations show that the strongest agents, such as Claude Opus 4.8, still perform well with limited views, while weaker agents degrade; customizing the environment thus adjusts task difficulty. The environment's verifiable rewards and restorable state also support training, and our Planner-Worker-Compressor harness cuts planner tokens by 57-61% by compacting the interaction history. We hope GeoGuesser Bench and its environment serve as a high-quality foundation for simulating real-world exploration at scale."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cf81be084d8bde949194bac0c95c372177c6de41", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03534", "CorpusId": 295502674}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cf81be084d8bde949194bac0c95c372177c6de41", "title": "An Agentic Framework for Automated Power System Studies and Grid Connection Assessment", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03534, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2338865516", "name": "Mao-Xun Huang"}, {"authorId": "1400855817", "name": "Kshitij Kapoor"}, {"authorId": "2526025971", "name": "Eli Louise"}], "abstract": "Connecting new generation and demand to the grid depends on two scarce skills: checking applications against a large, changing body of regulation, and running the network studies that decide where and how a project connects. We present Grid Agents, an AI system that brings both together, and the methodology we use to evaluate it. The Grid Ontology is its knowledge system. It represents the regulation, standards and engineering knowledge that grid work depends on, makes it searchable, and evolves as the agents learn from use. Every answer drawn from it rests on evidence the agents cite. Grid Studio is an agent system specialised for power-system engineering. It works in its own engineering environment with domain tools and a study framework, draws on the Grid Ontology, and relies on established solvers for every number it reports. In both, engineers make the decisions. We bring eight published power-system benchmark families, previously scattered across separate papers, into one suite of 519 tasks, and we verify not only each answer but the work behind it, by replaying the agent's tool calls. The Grid Ontology cites the right regulatory evidence more often than a general coding agent or retrieval pipelines, leads on LegalBench-RAG and ranks first on the public leaderboards of the Enterprise RAG Challenge and ViDoRe V3. Grid Studio scores highest on the 519 power-system tasks and is the fastest of all the systems we compare, both by a statistically significant margin. With an optimiser in the loop, its reinforcement packages cost 33-67% less on average than those the agent designs alone."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bca4f93228b896737fca503721cf09a2b611270e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03219", "CorpusId": 295504178}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bca4f93228b896737fca503721cf09a2b611270e", "title": "Can Coding Agents Sustain Continual Evolution without Forgetting?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03219, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362531413", "name": "Shangziqi Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2320286082", "name": "Yi-Tong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2380534443", "name": "Jia Li"}], "abstract": "Recently, harness self-evolution has emerged as an important paradigm for improving agent performance. Prior studies show that evolving the agent harness can effectively enhance agent capabilities without modifying the foundation model's parameters. We find that existing approaches largely study agent evolution on a single task. In practice, coding agents may need to handle multiple task types over time. Whether an agent harness can continuously evolve across multiple tasks without suffering from catastrophic forgetting remains an important open question. In this paper, we investigate this open question through a comprehensive empirical study of continual harness self-evolution. We organize three software engineering task datasets into six different evolution directions and apply two representative harness evolution methods to perform continual evolution along these directions. Our experimental results show that evolution on a subsequent dataset can substantially undermine the performance on previously seen datasets. For example, with DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731, Meta-Harness improves its performance on SWE-Bench to 94.0% after evolution. However, after further evolution on FeatureBench, its performance on SWE-Bench drops to 32.0% by the fifth round, corresponding to a 66% relative decrease. Finally, based on our experimental findings, we identify several insights, providing directions for the continual self-evolution of agent harnesses. Code is available at https://github.com/THU-Agent/continual-harness-evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "79b34fd69995fc7e76a9b26e7834d5bb632acfc6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02830", "CorpusId": 295503992}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/79b34fd69995fc7e76a9b26e7834d5bb632acfc6", "title": "Think with Skills: Pseudocode Planning Before Execution for Advanced Reasoners", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02830, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2392383684", "name": "Bo Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2322506329", "name": "Bo Yue"}, {"authorId": "2354789760", "name": "Xiang Li"}, {"authorId": "2268669", "name": "Shanchao Yang"}], "abstract": "Language model agents often struggle with complex tasks even when equipped with expert-written skills. Such skills encode declarative knowledge, whereas successful execution requires procedural knowledge tailored to the task at hand. Natural language skills may leave conditions and dependencies implicit, and tasks involving multiple skills further require agents to coordinate intermediate results and execution order. We introduce Think with Skills, a method that trains reasoning models to turn relevant skill descriptions into task-specific pseudocode plans before execution. The pseudocode explicitly represents operations, conditions, intermediate results, and dependencies, providing a structured bridge from skill descriptions to agent actions. We construct training data that pairs tasks and skills with pseudocode plans and execution trajectories, use supervised fine tuning to initialize this behavior, and further optimize end-to-end task performance with Group Relative Policy Optimization. On SkillsBench, Think with Skills raises task success from 35.0% to 62.0%. These results show that proceduralizing expert knowledge before execution substantially improves skill use."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "208b512dc4029baa34b3b2cb9bde4f92683c56ba", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03601", "CorpusId": 295503093}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/208b512dc4029baa34b3b2cb9bde4f92683c56ba", "title": "DiabAgent: Toward Agentic Physiological Control", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03601, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509349517", "name": "Cheng-Xi Li"}, {"authorId": "2509358622", "name": "Shao-Xuan Li"}, {"authorId": "2410898209", "name": "De-Yao Hong"}, {"authorId": "2445721255", "name": "Qing-Le Liu"}, {"authorId": "2320812021", "name": "Yiler Huang"}, {"authorId": "2526025912", "name": "Pu-Heng Bian"}, {"authorId": "2524801738", "name": "Min Xu"}, {"authorId": "2509365018", "name": "Lan-Yue Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2243451206", "name": "Ming-Ze Sun"}, {"authorId": "2513380315", "name": "Xin-Tong Li"}, {"authorId": "143940914", "name": "C. Xiao"}, {"authorId": "2455430554", "name": "Sheng-Yuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2371219369", "name": "Huan-Ang Gao"}, {"authorId": "2214082934", "name": "Jiazheng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2383097217", "name": "Kai-Sen Yang"}, {"authorId": "2253869803", "name": "Hong-Ning Wang"}], "abstract": "As pretraining scales, medical language models have advanced rapidly in clinical question answering. Their ability to achieve effective physiological control through sustained intervention still lacks systematic quantitative evaluation. Type 1 diabetes provides a concrete setting: impaired insulin secretion creates dependence on exogenous insulin, with dosing continually adjusted using information about meals, exercise, illness, and glucose measurements. We introduce DiabAgent, a research suite for agentic physiological control comprising the Agentic Physiological Control (APC) framework, the DiabetSim simulator, and the SweetBench benchmark. APC controls glucose through medical-device tools, using ongoing observations to infer individual states, accumulate personal experience, and choose treatment actions. We build DiabetSim to simulate different patients, events, and device conditions for scalable virtual testing and training. Using DiabetSim, we construct SweetBench by instantiating 32 scenario designs as 212 glucose-control tasks. With Claude Opus 5.5 and a fixed decision interval of one hour, APC achieves a COGI of 73.5, compared with 71.2 for the open-source closed-loop baseline OpenAPS. Ablations further show that individual adaptation and contextual information improve glucose control. Combining high-frequency rule-based control with lower-frequency agent decisions shows potential for balancing control performance and computational cost. Our work provides a framework, simulator, and evaluation foundation for agents to participate in human physiological control."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "09ac7074cc803c86adcdfd3d80593cb399f332b4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03618", "CorpusId": 295503160}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/09ac7074cc803c86adcdfd3d80593cb399f332b4", "title": "Strategist: Evolving Reasoning with Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03618, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2308418220", "name": "Taiming Lu"}, {"authorId": "2326321062", "name": "Qi Xu"}, {"authorId": "2105837204", "name": "Zhi-Gang Hua"}, {"authorId": "2349957935", "name": "Zhuang Liu"}], "abstract": "A model is only half of an AI agent system. The other half is the inference strategy: the reasoning procedure that turns model calls into a response. Today that second half is written by hand, and the experience of building one strategy never carries to the next. We introduce Strategist, a meta-agent that evolves inference strategies across tasks. Each strategy is written as a blueprint,executable code that other strategies can reuse, and together the blueprints form a library. Given a task and a small development set, Strategist retrieves related blueprints, writes candidate strategies, tests them on real samples, and revises them from the results. Every candidate then enters the library, so later tasks build on earlier discoveries. On 30 benchmarks, Strategist beats the strongest baseline on 24 and matches its average accuracy (74.7% vs.74.0%) at 39% lower inference cost. Its strategies also transfer to unseen tasks and other models without further search. The part of an AI system that is not improved on its own now does."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1a86f0e8a9007c9a6ab707daf11e57d6339ba55b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04003", "CorpusId": 295508185}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1a86f0e8a9007c9a6ab707daf11e57d6339ba55b", "title": "WASP-Mem: Let Agent Organize Procedural Memory. Human Spend Effort on Diagnosis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04003, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2305813566", "name": "Pei-Zhi Niu"}, {"authorId": "2387644739", "name": "Ge Shi"}, {"authorId": "2526101342", "name": "Vaibhav Gorde"}, {"authorId": "1500376988", "name": "Sarthak Ghosh"}, {"authorId": "2519134875", "name": "C. Faloutsos"}, {"authorId": "2398724957", "name": "Ming-Yue Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2526346047", "name": "Kevin Chen"}], "abstract": "LLM agents struggle to automate complex enterprise procedures because static, pre-built harness cannot capture proprietary policies, undocumented behaviors, or state-dependent recovery paths that surface only during execution. Procedural memory accrued from agent experience offers a training-free path to closing this gap -- yet unlike episodic records or semantic facts, it must consolidate repeated successes and diagnosed failures into reusable actions and recovery strategies. We introduce WASP-Mem (Wiki-based Agent-Synthesized Procedural Memory), a nimble but effective method that distills execution traces and feedback into persistent, interlinked wiki pages, enabling continual agent learning. On TheAgentCompany benchmark, WASP-Mem raises the checkpoint pass rate by 4.6-6.1% over the strongest baseline we compared across three models while requiring 57-61% less storage. We further present a systematic controlled study spanning consolidation method, feedback type, trace ingestion scope, and refinement rounds. Our results reveal that prescriptive knowledge organization such as human defined schemas and explicit deduplication rules hurts performance rather than closing the procedural-knowledge gap. In contrast, human diagnosis converts failed traces into corrective knowledge and drives sustained improvement: a single round of human diagnosis outperforms four rounds of self-judgment by 7.0-10.9%. These findings point to a simple division of labor: let agents organize procedural memory, and invest human effort where it matters most -- diagnosis."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1a6a255a29a8fa9f4c7611998bfc510a22233576", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03765", "CorpusId": 295506940}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1a6a255a29a8fa9f4c7611998bfc510a22233576", "title": "VagueBench: Benchmarking the Robustness of Embodied Agents on Ambient Context Illusions", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03765, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2342076508", "name": "Yang Qu"}, {"authorId": "2317148402", "name": "Qiang Fu"}, {"authorId": "2304955104", "name": "Zong-Hao Ying"}, {"authorId": "2526324007", "name": "Jia-Yi Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2380086966", "name": "Yao-Yuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2261414086", "name": "Jin-Yang Guo"}, {"authorId": "2399499739", "name": "Jian Yang"}, {"authorId": "2376423179", "name": "Ai-Shan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2526391764", "name": "Yin Wang"}, {"authorId": "2237942988", "name": "Xiang-Long Liu"}], "abstract": "Embodied agents are increasingly deployed in open-world environments, yet current robustness evaluations remain biased toward hazardous instructions and jailbreak vulnerabilities. A more fundamental issue is largely overlooked: agents are rarely tested under semantically sufficient but contextually minimal instructions (i.e., common in real-world human communication), where auxiliary contextual cues are absent. For the first, we identify a novel phenomenon termed Ambient Context Illusion, where agents fail to execute tasks correctly when non-essential contextual details are omitted, despite unchanged task semantics. To systematically study this, we establish VagueBench, a benchmark built upon the AI2-THOR interactive simulation environment for the evaluation of context dependency in embodied agents. We systematically consider key semantic components of instructions (entities, procedures, constraints, and prior knowledge) and derive a unified taxonomy with four categories and nine subtypes to reduce auxiliary information. VagueBench contains 1,303 instruction pairs across diverse indoor environments, constructed via a human-agent co-generation framework that integrates semantic redundancy estimation, taxonomy-guided transformation, and rigorous semantic-invariance verification. Through extensive evaluation on 6 state-of-the-art embodied agents, we observe significant degradation on VagueBench in both task performance and executability (relatively -19.65% in task success rate and relatively +54.10% in empty plan rate) revealing a strong reliance on auxiliary linguistic signals rather than core task semantics. Furthermore, we demonstrate that this phenomenon persists in real-world robotic systems, where it can lead to incorrect decisions and potential safety risks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e63c2a03c7b79972ea64fba61ee2fd4156fb2364", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1016/j.knosys.2026.117193", "CorpusId": 293063273}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e63c2a03c7b79972ea64fba61ee2fd4156fb2364", "title": "Giving stateless agents a sense of time: A reconsolidating episodic\u2013semantic memory architecture", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2026.117193", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1016/j.knosys.2026.117193?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2026.117193, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "102184028", "name": "G. Santi"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5e374252793d51fe2091f6378c3791ad06e0ec79", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1109/JIOT.2026.3729267", "CorpusId": 291670046}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5e374252793d51fe2091f6378c3791ad06e0ec79", "title": "Internet of Agents (IoA): Sensing, Communication, Decision-Making, and Execution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/jiot.2026.3729267", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/JIOT.2026.3729267?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/JIOT.2026.3729267, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2109492716", "name": "Jin-Qiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2271823433", "name": "Huan-Sheng Ning"}, {"authorId": "2111184450", "name": "Jian-Guo Ding"}, {"authorId": "2072832726", "name": "Tao Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2255361439", "name": "Mahmoud Daneshmand"}], "abstract": "Advances in sensing, communication, and reasoning are driving deeper integration of AI into IoT systems. However, progress remains siloed across two communities: agent research centers on task decomposition, semantic planning, and coordination, whereas IoT research prioritizes sensing, communication, and edge computing. Bridging these perspectives and integrating semantic reasoning with system foundations remains a key challenge. Therefore, we present the Internet of Agents (IoAs), a framework that reorganizes existing AI agent research from a systems perspective, following the classical IoT paradigm of sensing, communication, decision-making, and execution. IoA reconceptualizes system nodes as agents rather than traditional IoT endpoints. For sensing, IoA broadens awareness to include reasoning depth, knowledge gaps, and external tool capabilities. In communication, IoA adopts a three-layer architecture (environment, interaction, and semantic), designed to align with the five-layer Internet protocol stack. In decision-making and execution, IoA frames the shift from rule-based control to semantic planning and from reactive execution to proactive execution. We further identify open challenges and future research directions across the four modules of IoA."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a0be1e7c48baddf41925889139801ecc4ee0e217", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1016/j.telpol.2026.103339", "CorpusId": 294342014}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a0be1e7c48baddf41925889139801ecc4ee0e217", "title": "Liability for rogue behaviors of agentic AI", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308596126001898/pdf", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1016/j.telpol.2026.103339?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1016/j.telpol.2026.103339, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "1517670249", "name": "G. Papyshev"}, {"authorId": "2258436746", "name": "Keith Jin Deng Chan"}, {"authorId": "2267567288", "name": "Sara Migliorini"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c2d56e316057f3af150558ce5016c776c7f2f511", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02075", "CorpusId": 295501354}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c2d56e316057f3af150558ce5016c776c7f2f511", "title": "Decoupled Predictive Decision Making: A Shared Conditional Predictor for Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02075, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2305772842", "name": "Lang Mo"}, {"authorId": "51258901", "name": "Xing-Xuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2516249082", "name": "Qi-Zhu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2378787223", "name": "Ming-Chao Hao"}, {"authorId": "2267905423", "name": "Hao Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2223763670", "name": "Chunjing Gan"}, {"authorId": "2154002462", "name": "Wang Lu"}, {"authorId": "2318046189", "name": "Peng Cui"}], "abstract": "Agent decisions depend on empirical relationships that deployment observations reveal and task descriptions alone may leave unresolved. We introduce Decoupled Predictive Decision Making (DPDM), an architecture that formulates heterogeneous agent decisions as conditional prediction with a shared pretrained learner. Language models provide semantic representations of current and historical records, while a general-purpose conditional predictor predicts task-defined targets from the represented evidence. Pretraining supplies reusable inference capability; deployment evidence instantiates problem-specific predictions without updating either model's neural parameters. Tool judgments, candidate preferences, and reasoning assessments share an evidence-and-query interface, with predictions mapped to choices by a decision rule. This architecture establishes complementary sources of agent capability: semantic knowledge acquired through language pretraining and empirical inference acquired through structured-data pretraining. A single frozen LimiX-2 predictor serves all five task categories. With Qwen3.5-27B as the semantic encoder, DPDM raises mean accuracy across 50 benchmarks from 78.80% with few-shot prompting to 84.92% under their respective evaluated history budgets, and reaches 82.33% with identical histories and queries. On ToolPRMBench's BFCL and ToolSandbox tasks, absolute accuracy gains are 34.26% and 28.87%, respectively, at the methods'respective evaluated history budgets. Accuracy improves as history grows. With identical imbalanced histories, mean balanced accuracy improves by an absolute 14.51% across five reasoning-step assessment tasks and four encoders. DPDM makes learning from experience an independently improvable foundation-model capability, creating a shared route for advances in conditional prediction to improve decisions across agent tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8263380a4e9d4b8c06dc0d744ef94b929c7b2dda", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01460", "CorpusId": 295501828}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8263380a4e9d4b8c06dc0d744ef94b929c7b2dda", "title": "Can Large Language Model Agents Learn by Watching Tutorial Videos?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01460, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2508880707", "name": "Hao-Yu Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2931718", "name": "Z. Fountas"}, {"authorId": "2360470978", "name": "Jun Wang"}], "abstract": "Humans continuously acquire knowledge from carefully crafted materials such as books and tutorial videos, carrying that understanding into future tasks. Millions of such instructional videos are uploaded online every day, yet LLM agents remain unable to exploit this ever-expanding wealth of human teaching. Their capabilities are frozen after training, while parameter updates at scale remain prohibitively expensive. This raises the central question: can LLM agents learn from this vast instructional resource without parameter updates? We introduce Tutorial Agentic Learning (TAL), a nonparametric continual-learning paradigm that consolidates tutorial videos into a persistent, structured tutorialbook memory through a two-stage adversarial process that harvests hard, video-grounded questions and uses them to refine the memory. At inference time, TAL retrieves through a Table-of-Contents (TOC) tree, tying retrieval directly to the model's reasoning rather than to embedding similarity. To study this setting, we introduce VidLearn, a multimodal QA benchmark built from 72 chess tutorial videos spanning 12 openings. On VidLearn, TAL outperforms all evaluated baselines and improves monotonically as more videos are consumed, achieving up to 113% and 91% relative gains over the corresponding vanilla MLLMs on the training and test splits, respectively. In actual gameplay, TAL reaches a stronger opening-phase position than its vanilla counterpart in 69% of head-to-head games. Evaluation in a second domain further demonstrates the generality of TAL. Together, these results highlight instructional videos as a scalable resource for parameter-update-free agent continual learning."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "84ffe36e332d6aec4316ccdeff4ac83abdc6a5ec", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01386", "CorpusId": 295502472}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/84ffe36e332d6aec4316ccdeff4ac83abdc6a5ec", "title": "Learning by Doing: Autonomously Compiling and Curating Agent Skills from Verified Traces", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01386, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2342283142", "name": "Daniel Chalef"}, {"authorId": "2526024728", "name": "Peter Evans"}, {"authorId": "2342283168", "name": "Pavlo Paliychuk"}, {"authorId": "2500514585", "name": "M. Patel"}, {"authorId": "2342284826", "name": "P. Rasmussen"}, {"authorId": "2004576551", "name": "Jack Ryan"}], "abstract": "Memory systems for production LLM agents mostly store semantic memory (facts about users and domains) or episodic memory (earlier interactions). Research systems such as Trace2Skill, WikiSkill, and SkillRevise learn procedures from agent runs, package them as Skills, and evaluate them on benchmarks. We describe Zep Agent Skills, a production service that gives deployed agents this procedural memory. The service uses only verified attempts as evidence, cites the supporting events for each field of a Skill, and governs the admission and distribution of each Skill version. The service records each task attempt as a Trace with ordered events, an outcome, and a verification strength. A compiler with separate Reflector and Curator roles turns verified Traces into versioned Skills. Scans and admission gates control which candidates become active, and verified use outcomes change the search rank of each Skill. The Agent Skills format is an open standard that Anthropic originated and donated to the Agentic AI Foundation, so any host of the format can load a compiled Skill. Agents retrieve Skills through lexical search with progressive disclosure, over an API or MCP. We evaluate Zep Agent Skills on $\\tau^3$-bench banking, AppWorld, and EnterpriseOps-Gym. Skills compiled from the Traces of a teacher model increase the pass rate of three weaker executor models on the same tasks, at every reasoning effort, by 4.5 to 18.8 points. Most of the gain is on the tasks that the teacher solved. On ALFWorld, Skills compiled from the runs of a small model give a gain similar to or larger than the gain that prior work reports for continuous fine-tuning, with no training."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6bd9ff9c3957cf1faac67fbce8b42650d82a52fc", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1016/j.orig.2026.11.001", "CorpusId": 295684969}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6bd9ff9c3957cf1faac67fbce8b42650d82a52fc", "title": "Materials world models: Foundation for scientific continuity, emergence, and creation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.orig.2026.11.001", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1016/j.orig.2026.11.001?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1016/j.orig.2026.11.001, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "145405835", "name": "Jie Xiong"}, {"authorId": "2108143493", "name": "Yong-Chang Liu"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Tong-Yi Zhang"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e3c249fb5a6fd71da369828d8348bf417047390f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.00518", "CorpusId": 295502083}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e3c249fb5a6fd71da369828d8348bf417047390f", "title": "Agentic Context Management for Multi-Turn Human-AI Conversations", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00518, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-31", "authors": [{"authorId": "2378956201", "name": "Matthew Ho"}, {"authorId": "2322638895", "name": "Cheng-Long Wang"}, {"authorId": "1780690", "name": "Baolin Peng"}, {"authorId": "1698689", "name": "N. Jojic"}, {"authorId": "2361509861", "name": "Philippe Laban"}, {"authorId": "2253458981", "name": "Michel Galley"}, {"authorId": "2256752511", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}, {"authorId": "2365503656", "name": "Lian-Hui Qin"}], "abstract": "LLMs degrade in multi-turn conversation partly because of context pollution: the model's own earlier outputs stay in context and anchor later turns, so an early misreading of the user's intent can persist after the user corrects it. Assistant omission (AO), dropping every assistant message, removes this pollution but fails when interaction is referential: later turns refer back to drafts, decisions, and tool-call results that exist only in assistant turns. We propose Agentic Conversation Context Curation (AC3), a training-free method in which a separate analyzer (1) consolidates the task specification from the user's messages alone, (2) audits the assistant's prior outputs against it, and (3) edits the context, either appending the analysis or discarding the assistant side and rebuilding the context from the specification. A second finding: pollution is contagious. An analyzer that sees the assistant's reasoning while extracting the specification can do worse than no intervention, and instructing it to ignore them did not close the gap, so we exclude assistant turns structurally. On Lost in Conversation (replaying difficulty-selected instances), where AO is an oracle by construction, AC3 closes 55-84% of the gap between full context and AO on three of four tasks and exceeds AO on the fourth, and an LLM that faithfully summarizes the history does not reproduce the gain even with more compute. On real WildChat conversations, a GPT-5-mini judge prefers AC3's next turn over both full context and AO in every configuration of our main table, though this preference depends on the judge; on CollabLLM results are mixed. In stateful tool use (tau2-bench), AO collapses to 0% on every model; AC3 does not collapse, though it falls slightly behind full context. Detector studies show that Reset removes injected pollution but is not span-selective: the gain comes from rebuilding the specification."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7222e92118ed1100a1ba4dcee474f309d336943f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01351", "CorpusId": 295502394}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7222e92118ed1100a1ba4dcee474f309d336943f", "title": "MAPLE: A Multimodal Mobile Agent with Playbook-Learned Experience", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01351, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-31", "authors": [{"authorId": "2267697061", "name": "Jun Wang"}, {"authorId": "2242967654", "name": "A. Wei"}, {"authorId": "2269472581", "name": "L. Spangher"}, {"authorId": "2290594698", "name": "Ming Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2306992351", "name": "Eugene Ie"}, {"authorId": "3092871", "name": "Yiannis Kantaros"}], "abstract": "Autonomous agents powered by vision-language models (VLMs) show great potential in navigating mobile graphical user interfaces (GUIs) to complete complex tasks. However, existing paradigms face fundamental scalability bottlenecks: fine-tuning requires massive high-fidelity datasets or prohibitive training time and compute, while gradient-free approaches rely on custom static heuristics or costly trial-and-error trajectory exploration where feedback is deferred until the end of execution. To address these limitations, we introduce MAPLE (Multimodal Agent with Playbook-Learned Experience), a novel gradient-free multi-agent framework that enables dynamic, step-wise experience sharing and execution-time prompt evolution. Unlike traditional action-reflection architectures that depend on costly, post-hoc self-reflection, MAPLE enables agents to cross-reference and distribute runtime insights in real time at each operational step. This dynamic exchange eliminates blind trial-and-error looping and steers agents along direct, grounded interaction paths. Extensive evaluations on the AndroidWorld and MobileWorld benchmarks demonstrate that MAPLE achieves state-of-the-art performance on complex, long-horizon mobile tasks, while proving scalable and adaptable across novel environments."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3d1ff0065d0d0b2d11928c64d7953304b19080f5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.41216", "CorpusId": 295402364}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3d1ff0065d0d0b2d11928c64d7953304b19080f5", "title": "TinyAstral: Transferring Self-Evolving Harnesses to Tiny Generalist Manipulation Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41216, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2397760867", "name": "Ke-Hong Gong"}, {"authorId": "1753669202", "name": "Xiao-Xu Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2437476734", "name": "Guan-Ling Hou"}, {"authorId": "2525389666", "name": "Seung-Hun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2507875926", "name": "Meng-Qi Guo"}, {"authorId": "151506836", "name": "Run-Jia Tan"}, {"authorId": "2398983002", "name": "Zheng-Yu Wen"}, {"authorId": "2398589143", "name": "Qi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2274713895", "name": "Xiao-Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2397823150", "name": "Ming-Yuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "153122790", "name": "Michael Bi Mi"}], "abstract": "Robot agents built on vision-language models depend on both the planner and its harness: the tools, feedback, memory, and recovery logic around it. Can improvements to a large teacher's harness be carried into a compact agent with lower inference compute cost? We present TinyAstral, a two-stage approach. We first freeze an open-weight teacher and improve its harness through recursive self-improvement (RSI): frontier LLM agents read the teacher's trajectories and propose harness edits, and each edit is accepted only if it passes a paired statistical test. We then freeze the final harness, collect teacher trajectories with reasoning and failure recovery, and distill a small student that replaces the teacher in the same harness. On RoboTwin, harness evolution raises the teacher's success from 33.4% to 63.6%; the final 4B student reaches 62.8%. Across four harness versions, distilled 4B students score close to their corresponding teachers, and later versions yield stronger students, showing that harness evolution gains carry into compact models. The harness gains also transfer to other frozen planners without retraining. On identical episodes, the final 4B student outperforms open 4B agents, the open 27B harness-RSI model SenseNova-RoboRSI, and our own teacher model placed in three competitors'harnesses. It stays close to its teacher on all three training benchmarks, and on three benchmarks never used for RSI or training it scores close to a 552B planner running the same harness. On the measured deployment episodes, it uses about one fifth of its teacher's accelerator time."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a0dac219b30d06086741ab87aabc773d191a5342", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.41350", "CorpusId": 295402474}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a0dac219b30d06086741ab87aabc773d191a5342", "title": "Operon-S1: a fast decision controller for long-horizon biomedical agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41350, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2525174799", "name": "Genoria AI Team Yuejie Hou"}, {"authorId": "2145217203", "name": "Yueyuxiao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2269784284", "name": "Wei-Ting Tang"}, {"authorId": "25518906", "name": "Meng-Cheng Yang"}], "abstract": "Language-model agents now carry out long scientific work in biology, but their judgements about conventions, skills, errors, evidence and stopping are made inside the reasoning that does the work, where they cannot be measured, replaced or improved on their own. Here we present Operon-S1, a fast decision system, a System 1 for the agent, that separates judging from doing. A decision harness poses the agent's recurring judgements as closed questions at 13 decision points and acts only through fixed messages that never contain an answer, so any model that answers the questions can serve as the judge. Across seven life-science benchmarks, a local decision model took over these judgements in 0.6 s at the median for an in-loop request on CPU at no request cost, with no measurable change in the agent's score on five of six benchmarks, whereas the general language model that drives the agent, asked the same questions, accounted for 8.0% to 35.2% of task cost. Every decision is recorded with its consequence. Retraining on labels from task construction, verifiers or execution raised decision accuracy on every question in each of three trained versions, and a new version enters service only through a promotion gate. Task-level results in this study use the public checkpoint. The same closed decisions serve wet-laboratory protocol troubleshooting and the design of a germline target enrichment panel. Like the regulator of an operon, which switches genes on and off without making their products, Operon-S1 steers the agent without doing its work, and makes the agent's judgements a layer that can be measured, replaced and trained."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b94b81c3a922bda9acc99f202d8c2464a08dcf2c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.41143", "CorpusId": 295401794}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b94b81c3a922bda9acc99f202d8c2464a08dcf2c", "title": "TalentRL: Strategy-Guided Reinforcement Learning for Hierarchical Skill Routing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41143, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2467997082", "name": "Huan-Cheng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2516254148", "name": "Xi Sun"}, {"authorId": "2506338691", "name": "Zhao-Qiong Huang"}, {"authorId": "2525176614", "name": "Michelle Chang"}, {"authorId": "2301157480", "name": "Jing-Wen Lu"}], "abstract": "Natural-language skills enable large language model (LLM) agents to reuse procedures across tasks, yet most systems retrieve individual skills from a flat SkillBank without explicitly modeling the high-level strategy that governs their selection and use. We introduce TalentRL, a strategy-guided reinforcement learning framework for hierarchical routing over procedural memory. Coarse-grained talents represent recurring strategies for skill use, while fine-grained skills provide executable procedures. For each task, the policy model selects a talent that guides execution and softly prioritizes its associated skills during similarity-based retrieval. However, since task outcomes depend jointly on talent selection, retrieved skill context, and downstream execution, terminal rewards alone cannot isolate the contribution of the routing decision. TalentRL therefore trains talent routing with reward contrasts between rollouts of the same task under matched interventions on the talent-conditioned context, and optimizes downstream execution with group-relative advantages. Across seven open-domain and multi-hop question-answering benchmarks, talent-aware supervised fine-tuning improves average success rate by 7.53%, 6.71%, and 9.21% over the corresponding zero-shot baselines for Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen3-8B, and Llama-3.1-8B, respectively. Reinforcement learning further improves these SFT checkpoints by 7.58% to 9.32%, and TalentRL achieves the highest average success rate among all compared methods on all three backbones. On the agentic benchmarks ALFWorld and WebShop, TalentRL achieves the highest ALFWorld success rate (99.3%) and the highest WebShop score (90.7) among all compared methods."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6174320091cd2782466817da67e55a7a318fc3b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.41809", "CorpusId": 295402308}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6174320091cd2782466817da67e55a7a318fc3b9", "title": "Continual Reinforcement Learning Is an Integration Problem", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41809, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2523374095", "name": "Nis Meinert"}, {"authorId": "2051292610", "name": "Jakob Gawlikowski"}, {"authorId": "2485707995", "name": "Eddie Grant"}, {"authorId": "2525173991", "name": "Max Cyprys"}, {"authorId": "1745611", "name": "N. Schurr"}, {"authorId": "2525174853", "name": "Neil Boston"}, {"authorId": "2525174130", "name": "Alfredo Ibias"}, {"authorId": "2288282318", "name": "Milind Tambe"}, {"authorId": "1995313", "name": "J. Marecki"}], "abstract": "This paper presents an opinionated set of ten desiderata intended to inform the design and evaluation of reinforcement-learning agents that learn continually throughout a continuing lifetime. We use these desiderata to examine a representative selection of existing approaches. Progress on individual desiderata is valuable, but the longer-term aim should be to integrate all ten within a single agent. To our knowledge, no published evaluation has yet provided evidence for such an integration. Likewise, the representative benchmarks we review probe different parts of this target, but separate evaluations do not establish that the corresponding properties coexist within one agent throughout a continuing lifetime. As a secondary contribution, we therefore present a compact persistent-world scenario as a playable companion to the desiderata and outline a proposed benchmark that examines a broad range of associated behavioral capabilities together within one such lifetime."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b8b550b3f273feb365dd3b94f5d1564b068bd0d7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.42053", "CorpusId": 295402080}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b8b550b3f273feb365dd3b94f5d1564b068bd0d7", "title": "REA: A Routed Embodied Agent Runtime for Asynchronous Brain-Cerebellum Control", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.42053, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2332683169", "name": "P. Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2345327636", "name": "Yi-Xin Ma"}, {"authorId": "2459570212", "name": "Zhengfeiyang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2521031081", "name": "Hao-Yuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2325006710", "name": "Zheng-Lin Wan"}, {"authorId": "2451648916", "name": "He-Song Wang"}, {"authorId": "2292217857", "name": "Wang-Bo Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2282982772", "name": "Yang You"}], "abstract": "Foundation models are becoming remarkably reliable at high-level reasoning, yet they remain too slow and costly to serve as fine-grained controllers. Conversely, fast policies like VLAs and whole-body controllers offer more efficient reflexes, but struggle to generalize. While pairing a deliberative brain (foundation models) with a reactive cerebellum (fast policies) seems natural for embodied systems, two obstacles remain: methods are built and scored on incompatible stacks, and their asynchronous coordination is difficult. For example, an agent that calls the VLA in the agentic loop can stall the fast execution, while a VLA that calls the agent on errors tends to re-trigger fallbacks repeatedly or completely miss silent failures. To address this, we propose REA (Routed Embodied Agent), a unified runtime where a deliberative System 2, an embodied router System 1 and reactive System 0 policies cooperate under a single golden rule - no running policy waits on the brain. Instead of querying the brain at each failure, REA employs a route memory that stores each policy's measured success ahead of time, a boundary-triggered judge gate that consults the brain on policy execution, and a shared-budget escalation ladder for bounded recovery. Evaluated on 3,000 test episodes across 60 LIBERO-Pro tasks, REA reaches 95.9% success and beats the best single-agent baseline in its own policy pool (94.7%) while reducing control steps by 25% and control time by 17%. In a separate 100-episode RoboTwin 2.0 study, REA achieves 84% success with VLA policies and 86% with VLA+code, compared with 80% for the strongest single VLA baseline MEDex. These results demonstrate learned and programmatic policies operating within the same runtime. REA also transfers to BEHAVIOR-1K and a real Unitree G1 humanoid, achieving the highest observed success among the compared methods."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0b42caed4a57c6db23ab2d09c7342d677996b8a5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.41378", "CorpusId": 295402100}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0b42caed4a57c6db23ab2d09c7342d677996b8a5", "title": "Ask on Demand: Targeted Peer Communication for Parallel Agentic Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41378, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2360716996", "name": "Hao-Fu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2525380048", "name": "Hong-Yang Yin"}, {"authorId": "2525172850", "name": "Ran Bi"}, {"authorId": "2393709490", "name": "Fa-Guo Wu"}, {"authorId": "2525172895", "name": "Peng Xue"}, {"authorId": "2516985941", "name": "Feng-Yu Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2483018394", "name": "Han-Mo Zhang"}], "abstract": "Parallel LLM agents tackle complex tasks by exploring multiple trajectories, yet useful discoveries remain isolated in individual execution histories. Existing sharing methods often select or summarize information before other agents'needs become clear, potentially overlooking details that become important later. We propose Ask on Demand (AOD), a communication framework that enables agents to seek targeted peer assistance when specific information needs arise. Upon an approved request, AOD retrieves relevant evidence from peer execution histories, selects a suitable peer, and generates a response tailored to the request. To determine when such assistance is worthwhile, we train a lightweight gate using paired team continuations, with supervision derived from final team outcomes and inference cost. Experiments on AppWorld and WebShop demonstrate that AOD consistently improves task performance over independent parallel execution while achieving lower inference cost than existing information-sharing baselines. These findings highlight the benefits of aligning peer communication with agents'evolving information needs to achieve more effective and efficient collaboration."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7e3bf5b905d9b18ff3966f6d14f1f3419a9ce588", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.00180", "CorpusId": 295503090}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7e3bf5b905d9b18ff3966f6d14f1f3419a9ce588", "title": "Learning to Live: A Step Toward Lifelong Embodied Intelligence", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00180, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2423412076", "name": "Li-Rong Che"}, {"authorId": "2282963978", "name": "Pei-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2486727700", "name": "Yi-Meng Su"}, {"authorId": "2507855939", "name": "Ji-Aan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2473776641", "name": "Yang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2353301589", "name": "Wen-Bo Fu"}, {"authorId": "2354550156", "name": "Zhen-Yu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2274200186", "name": "Tian-Cheng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2316673843", "name": "Yuzhe Yang"}, {"authorId": "2526026216", "name": "Dong-Li Xie"}, {"authorId": "2467100130", "name": "Xu Cao"}, {"authorId": "2345700914", "name": "Chuang Wang"}, {"authorId": "34776599", "name": "Da-Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2430939627", "name": "Jiang-Feng Su"}], "abstract": "Embodied agents are usually organized around individual tasks, yet the physical world keeps changing between tasks and during execution. We introduce LIFE (Lifelong Intelligence from Experience), a framework in which a robot observes continuously, remembers selectively, and turns experience into new capabilities. RoboBeats, a family of lightweight streaming gates, tracks conditions set by the agent and discovers new events, deciding when an observation warrants a response or a memory write. Selected observations enter Plastic Memory, a flat store of images and structured records that keeps the source evidence, so new fields and indexes can be added when later questions need them. When existing tools fall short, Explore-Distill-Mount (EDM) turns solutions found during problem solving into validated, reusable tools and skills. Under matched protocols, RoboBeats substantially improves condition tracking over its base models and, at the same writing budget, retains more useful evidence for memory question answering than uniform sampling. A case study of limited scale, replaying 24 hours of continuous video recorded by a real robot, illustrates how the integrated system builds memory from a day of experience and uses it to answer everyday questions. LIFE takes a step toward robots that become more familiar with their surroundings and more capable through experience: learning not only to complete tasks, but to live in the world."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6c9500677dcc7e325a53f16f08c915fa2e8b8522", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.40747", "CorpusId": 295402627}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6c9500677dcc7e325a53f16f08c915fa2e8b8522", "title": "Mage-VL-Cowork: A Full Training Recipe to Learn Hybrid Computer Use From Evolved Harnesses", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40747, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2258309879", "name": "Xiao-Yi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2109293550", "name": "Hao-Qing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2448364618", "name": "Xin-Jie Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2463123531", "name": "Ming Xiang"}, {"authorId": "2525382989", "name": "Xing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2276746547", "name": "Sen-Qiao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2386208144", "name": "Kai-Chen Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2268292391", "name": "Zhao-Yang Jia"}, {"authorId": "2304753124", "name": "An-Dong Tan"}, {"authorId": "2262278343", "name": "Xin-Jiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2450735273", "name": "Jing-Hao Guo"}, {"authorId": "2331895075", "name": "Yi-Fei Shen"}, {"authorId": "2108787238", "name": "Xiao Li"}, {"authorId": "2351684838", "name": "Peng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2372345805", "name": "Xun Guo"}, {"authorId": "2108959570", "name": "Jia-Hao Li"}, {"authorId": "2249035445", "name": "Shi-Cheng Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2364801583", "name": "Jing-Lu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2363407109", "name": "Zong-Yu Guo"}, {"authorId": "2257012595", "name": "Wen-Xuan Xie"}, {"authorId": "2185912245", "name": "Bin Li"}, {"authorId": "2355689676", "name": "Yan Lu"}], "abstract": "Effective computer-use agents (CUAs) should seamlessly combine visual GUI interaction with code and terminal execution within a single policy. We present Mage-VL-Cowork, a 4B hybrid CUA trained from a general multimodally aligned checkpoint through an end-to-end, progressive five-stage curriculum across midtraining, SFT, and RL. To power this curriculum at scale, Mage-CUA-Forge replaces per-task agent construction with reusable, skill-conditioned generation workflows and application-specific preparers, synthesizing 293,806 desktop and web tasks across 6,915 skills while reducing recurring per-task expenditure by 82.6%. Rather than directly distilling a default teacher, we evolve the teacher harness and instantiate specialized CUA harnesses to yield high-quality trajectories alongside stage-specific supervision. Mage-VL-Cowork-4B finally climbs to 66.0% on OSWorld-Verified through different training stages with more efficient reasoning cost, achieving state-of-the-art performance at its parameter size and pushing forward the frontier of on-device intelligence."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d5ac091c19e2adc4946bfd9a7083f78133fc311c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.40227", "CorpusId": 295372071}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d5ac091c19e2adc4946bfd9a7083f78133fc311c", "title": "EpiAgent: Epistemic Self-Play for LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40227, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2303980025", "name": "Xuan-Fa Jin"}, {"authorId": "2262430027", "name": "Xin-Yu Cui"}, {"authorId": "2238083644", "name": "Zhi-Jian Ma"}, {"authorId": "2262502320", "name": "Xue Yan"}, {"authorId": "2305155346", "name": "Jian Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2247479851", "name": "Hai-Feng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2386604138", "name": "Jun Wang"}], "abstract": "Epistemic game theory explains an agent's strategic behavior via the beliefs it holds about others and how these beliefs are updated as the interactions unfold. Modern LLM-based agents, however, often lack an explicit mechanism for learning and updating such beliefs to guide decisions under incomplete information. To bridge this gap, we propose EpiAgent, a framework that equips LLM-based agents with an explicit, learnable epistemic layer, which maintains beliefs over others'private information and combines these beliefs with the agent's available information into an epistemic state for action selection. Concretely, the agent's policy is decomposed into two coupled stages: Epistemic Update, which updates these beliefs from interaction histories, and Action Planning, which selects actions conditioned on the updated epistemic state. By formulating their dependency as a bi-level optimization problem, we introduce Epistemic Self-Play, an algorithm that alternately optimizes the two stages through self-play, utilizing an intrinsic prediction reward to evaluate how well inferred beliefs predict subsequent observations. Empirical results across both mixed and competitive scenarios show that EpiAgent consistently outperforms prompting-based and RL-based baselines. Further analyses reveal the benefits of dedicated epistemic training, improvements in belief inference accuracy, and the contribution of learned beliefs to action planning. These results demonstrate the value of an explicit, learnable epistemic layer for effective multi-agent interactions."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8324342d13c75d45a77095dbcd349fc180a31ee6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.39180", "CorpusId": 295372061}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8324342d13c75d45a77095dbcd349fc180a31ee6", "title": "EmbodiExp: Compositional Experience Transfer for Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39180, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2374380016", "name": "Zhi-Feng Gu"}, {"authorId": "2287862878", "name": "Ying Chen"}, {"authorId": "2316411779", "name": "Rui-En Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2268566173", "name": "Lei Yi"}, {"authorId": "2505536765", "name": "Bing-Ning Wang"}], "abstract": "Experience can help embodied agents turn high-level plans into grounded actions, but transferring it across tasks remains challenging: previously effective strategies may be inapplicable to the current state or fail to produce their intended outcomes. We present EmbodiExp, a framework for constructing transferable experiences and composing them online during embodied task execution. Our key observation is that tasks often share subgoals whose completion can be assessed independently of overall task success. We therefore introduce an outcome-grounded subgoal experience that specifies a subgoal, applicability conditions, an adaptable policy, and a verifiable outcome. During execution, applicable experiences guide action generation, while verified outcomes can establish the conditions required by subsequent experiences, enabling online composition. Subgoal-level outcome attribution further enables failure utilization: failed trajectories can still contribute verified local successes and condition-specific guidance on actions to avoid. Experiments on multiple embodied benchmarks under a fold-based protocol with frozen experience banks demonstrate the effectiveness of EmbodiExp."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "900bbccd5bb34ebd68a400a3772f0f9eccd0305f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.39122", "CorpusId": 295373286}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/900bbccd5bb34ebd68a400a3772f0f9eccd0305f", "title": "Appreciation Teaches Unauthorized Execution: Self-Evolution Degrades the Robustness of Personal Agents to Indirect Prompt Injection", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39122, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2282913158", "name": "Xin-Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2257096300", "name": "Xiaogeng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2524789697", "name": "Jun Shao"}, {"authorId": "2273519476", "name": "Chao-Wei Xiao"}], "abstract": "Self-evolving personal agents act for users and learn from user feedback. However, if an agent obeys an instruction that an untrusted third party hides in content, the third party gains control of the agent. Such indirect prompt injection (IPI) poses severe risks. We investigate whether self-evolution degrades the robustness of personal agents to IPI. To study this question, we design a controlled two-phase methodology. In the adaptation phase, the user asks the agent to read external content and complete the task described in it, a common request in daily use. The agent then learns from the user's feedback and improves itself as designed. In the test phase, a third party writes a similar task into new external content, but the user's test request does not authorize the injected task. Using this methodology, we build multiple tasks with diverse test instances in different domains for a real personal agent. Our extensive experiments reveal that self-evolution substantially degrades robustness to IPI. Specifically, compared with the base agent, which does not evolve from user feedback, the average attack success rate rises by 14.0--48.8 percentage points under skill evolution and by 3.0--39.3 percentage points under reinforcement learning, posing safety risks. The code is available at https://github.com/SaFo-Lab/Evolve-IPI"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0eda360c6e31e4f56738a6caaad8eeab724e6ebf", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.39813", "CorpusId": 295373657}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0eda360c6e31e4f56738a6caaad8eeab724e6ebf", "title": "EvoTeam: Multi-Agent Collaboration via Evolving Teams", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39813, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2524779955", "name": "Qi-Nuo Cao"}, {"authorId": "2405881731", "name": "Xin He"}, {"authorId": "2353328270", "name": "Dian-Xuan Fu"}, {"authorId": "2508438582", "name": "Li-Zhi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2524787057", "name": "Zhao-Qing Xing"}, {"authorId": "2163833353", "name": "Jia-Pu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2447875331", "name": "Xin Wang"}, {"authorId": "49648991", "name": "Na Zou"}, {"authorId": "2362528114", "name": "Yi Chang"}, {"authorId": "2172162773", "name": "Qing-Gang Zhang"}], "abstract": "LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) rely on collaboration among specialized agents to solve complex tasks. Existing methods improve MAS by refining the prompts, skills, and execution strategies of individual agents based on task feedback. However, improving individual agents does not necessarily lead to better team collaboration. Recent studies have therefore extended evolution to the collaboration level, using collective execution experience to adjust team organization, and inter-agent interactions. Nevertheless, evolving collaboration across heterogeneous tasks remains challenging, as existing approaches often constrain team reorganization within a shared structure and reuse interaction rules across incompatible team contexts. In this paper, we propose EvoTeam, a collaboration-centric evolution framework that jointly evolves team organization, interaction rules, and individual agent skills through execution experience. It maintains a library of reusable teams, selecting or creating agent teams and guiding collaboration through team-specific interaction rules. Collaboration experience accumulates within compatible team contexts, while individual skills remain reusable across teams. Experiments across four benchmarks demonstrate its effectiveness. Our code is available at https://github.com/DEEP-JLU/EvoTeam."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b19b0b08c501e09f00f772fb5dbf204b340f79c9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.39380", "CorpusId": 295373904}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b19b0b08c501e09f00f772fb5dbf204b340f79c9", "title": "Learning to Formulate: Agentic Hardware-Software Co-Design of AI Infrastructure through Progressive Optimization and Verification", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39380, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515996173", "name": "Yi Ding"}, {"authorId": "2065825344", "name": "Li Shang"}], "abstract": "AI infrastructure, the chips and software that train and serve AI models, is designed in layers. Layers make the system manageable, but they also fix which choices an optimizer can consider. Gains that need the chip and its software to change together are excluded before optimization begins: the formulation leaves them out. Language-model agents make it possible to revisit these boundaries, but an agent's proposal cannot show by itself that a design is correct or optimal. We present Formulaic, a flow that divides the work. The agent writes the formulation, a mathematical solver optimizes within it, and verification and measurement check its assumptions and results. The agent refines the problem step by step, from the computation graph toward the chip. Each step is a template: a family of programs with the conditions under which they are correct. Wall relaxation searches the chip by pricing the hardware limits that bind the software. This paper takes a first step, through separate studies rather than one end-to-end run. On NVIDIA GB10, templates the agent wrote run complete Qwen3-8B layers $1.075\\times$ faster than SGLang. In a simulated Ventus GPU study, joint design cuts the worst-scenario cycles of Qwen2.5-0.5B to 0.428 of the reference, against 0.934 for software alone. On a five-step GEMM refinement chain, reusing proofs makes checking $2.51\\times$ faster."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "005a04ba4c307d52051a3d19352197d8aab80812", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.40565", "CorpusId": 295371813}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/005a04ba4c307d52051a3d19352197d8aab80812", "title": "Gym-Anything for Robotics: Scaling physical simulations with digital agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40565, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2114841965", "name": "Pranjal Aggarwal"}, {"authorId": "2129663", "name": "S. Welleck"}], "abstract": "Realistic, fast robotics simulations could enable large-scale training of embodied AI models that transfer to the real world. However, despite decades of progress, available simulations cover only a narrow range of real-world tasks and physical phenomena. The core bottleneck is that creating better simulations requires significant expert effort and therefore does not scale. To address this, we introduce Gym-Anything for Robotics, a framework in which coding agents create realistic robotics simulations. Our core insight is that simulation creation is a coding agent task, so improvements in coding agents translate directly into richer and more faithful simulations. We select simulations based on U.S. GDP data to cover economically valuable physical work, define what makes a good simulation and a good task, and develop an adversarial creation-audit loop that checks each simulation and task against these definitions. Using this pipeline, we build RoboWorld, a set of 25 simulations with 25 test and 175 training RL environments of varying difficulty. Even the best frontier models fail on every RoboWorld test task, achieving an average reward of only 0.06. Building on RoboWorld, we introduce several new ways for frontier models to improve, including in-context learning across repeated attempts, a privileged teacher that reads the simulator state and teaches a student in context, self-assisted learning from the model's own privileged experience, and code policies that generate demonstrations across varied scenes to train vision-language-action models (VLAs) such as $\\pi_{0.5}$. Each of these substantially improves performance. We release our pipeline, RL environments, and benchmark to facilitate future research. Code and data are available at https://gym-anything.github.io/robotics"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0cc3ad86ab4214a3be568018ea7e5e3a32ff4a97", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.40276", "CorpusId": 295373399}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0cc3ad86ab4214a3be568018ea7e5e3a32ff4a97", "title": "TaskFrame: Connecting Task-Level Planning with Frame-Level Control for Game Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40276, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2392993796", "name": "Guang-Zhi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2310610275", "name": "Junfu Pu"}, {"authorId": "2502200984", "name": "Yi-Ran Wang"}, {"authorId": "2275595907", "name": "Xingyilang Yin"}, {"authorId": "2502200470", "name": "Hong-Hao Chen"}, {"authorId": "2502176479", "name": "Cheng Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2243322951", "name": "Lin-Gen Li"}, {"authorId": "2333350491", "name": "Yi-Nan Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2350428628", "name": "Jia-Rui Wu"}, {"authorId": "2157838086", "name": "Yi-Nuo Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2362310872", "name": "Hong-Bo Jin"}, {"authorId": "2355422738", "name": "Xiaodong Cun"}, {"authorId": "2311884929", "name": "Ying Shan"}], "abstract": "Building general game agents requires both high-level task reasoning and low-latency action control in rapidly changing visual environments. These capabilities favor models with substantially different computational profiles, making them difficult to realize within a single model. In this work, we present TaskFrame, a hierarchical planner-controller framework that connects task-level planning with frame-level keyboard-and-mouse control. A vision-language task planner decomposes a high-level goal into an ordered sequence of atomic instructions and is optimized through supervised learning and dependency-aware GRPO. The hidden states of special goal tokens that terminate each atomic instruction provide continuous goal embeddings that are cached and reused across control steps. A causal Transformer controller combines the embedding of the active goal with recent observations to predict discrete keyboard and mouse-button actions and continuous camera motion at every game step. We train the system in three stages: planner training, controller pretraining, and joint alignment. Experiments in Minecraft show that TaskFrame is competitive with general-purpose vision-language models on instruction decomposition, and that in closed-loop evaluation the agent achieves a higher combat success rate than the evaluated baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f3b1c21945586fe38f7b0c063cb1352b8b633fdc", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38266", "CorpusId": 295372300}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3b1c21945586fe38f7b0c063cb1352b8b633fdc", "title": "Recursive Self-Improvement in Physical AI: Mechanisms, Evidence, and Evaluation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38266, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2353440123", "name": "Xiao-Feng Han"}, {"authorId": "2524751956", "name": "Kai-Ze Luo"}, {"authorId": "2354260566", "name": "Zeng-Huang Fu"}, {"authorId": "2524803248", "name": "Zi-Chong Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2353852983", "name": "Zhe Feng"}, {"authorId": "1490749570", "name": "Changwei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2106213769", "name": "Rongtao Xu"}, {"authorId": "35965884", "name": "Wei-Liang Meng"}, {"authorId": "2386930994", "name": "Xiao-Peng Zhang"}], "abstract": "Embodied systems can update policies, world models, skills, and executable programs from deployment experience, allowing one interaction to benefit later tasks. When a system also revises and reuses its data-acquisition, feedback, or learning procedures, experience changes the process that produces capability, introducing recursive self-improvement. This paper surveys research in physical AI through the connection between persistent capability and its improvement process. We first explain the functional distinction between learner updates and improvers, then compare policy learning, imagined experience, memory and skill accumulation, and executable harnesses. These approaches connect to revisions of curricula, rewards, and research programs in different ways. Contact validity, recovery, retention, and resource costs determine whether the resulting gains accumulate during continued deployment. An L0-L3 rubric distinguishes task-local adaptation, persistent capability gain, inherited process revision, and verified improver gain. Representative benchmarks and within-study contrasts relate these distinctions to existing results. We further propose common-data and interactive comparisons that evaluate reference and revised improvers from shared learner starts, separating the use of experience from its acquisition. This perspective connects the diverse methods, physical operating conditions, and evaluation evidence, underpinning embodied RSI. A comprehensive list of studies in this survey is available at https://github.com/nkd-lkz/physical-ai-rsi-survey."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "20525c1d5303236623a317b17e75654feb8fc0a2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38796", "CorpusId": 295373346}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/20525c1d5303236623a317b17e75654feb8fc0a2", "title": "Routed Continual Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38796, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2386914840", "name": "Michael Philip"}, {"authorId": "2262918969", "name": "M. Balakrishnan"}, {"authorId": "4840930", "name": "D. Geraghty"}, {"authorId": "2428627302", "name": "David Mao"}, {"authorId": "2133380214", "name": "Vidhya Venkat"}, {"authorId": "2065193618", "name": "Ilya Mironov"}], "abstract": "Agents deployed at scale repeatedly encounter related problems, and it is natural to let them learn from each other's experience rather than starting over each time. Unfortunately, there is no single way to retain that experience that works well across settings, and no way to know in advance which future problems a given experience will help with. A global history preserves every detail but becomes expensive and mixes together experience that may not belong together; compressing that history into one fixed representation limits the cost but can throw away valuable details. We introduce Routed Continual Learning (Routed CL), an architecture that decides how to partition and retain experience. Past trajectories are grouped into clusters, each of which maintains state using one memory system from a fixed library. Before each run, an LLM-based router either reuses an existing cluster or creates a new one, basing its decision on the problem statement, memory system descriptions, and cluster summaries. We also introduce Context Memory Adapter, which mediates between the memory system and the agentic loop. We evaluate the architecture on a heterogeneous stream that interleaves 301 instances from the six CL-Bench tasks with 97 banking instances from $\\tau^3$-Bench. Across four language models and five matched rollouts, Routed Continual Learning raises normalized reward over a single global in-context learning (ICL) history by 15-30% of the maximum attainable normalized reward, achieves higher mean normalized reward than ICL-only clustering, and reduces estimated API cost by 65-72% relative to Global ICL. These results support approaching continual learning as a routing problem that chooses both a learning mechanism and a state cluster rather than committing to one representation globally."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "52238b4833a88f1649da08b7e1dada5cb1ae7d5d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38391", "CorpusId": 295372710}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/52238b4833a88f1649da08b7e1dada5cb1ae7d5d", "title": "SCOPE: Selective Critical-Step On-Policy Self-Distillation for Agentic Reinforcement Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38391, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2491574253", "name": "Qi-Rui Liu"}, {"authorId": "2502152235", "name": "Wei Cao"}, {"authorId": "2400279583", "name": "Yu Mi"}, {"authorId": "2300306317", "name": "Yi-Chen Sun"}, {"authorId": "2524820452", "name": "Xue-Kun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2326922496", "name": "Lei Liu"}, {"authorId": "2290138094", "name": "Yue Shen"}, {"authorId": "2303254578", "name": "Zhi-Xuan Chu"}], "abstract": "Outcome-based reinforcement learning provides coarse supervision for long-horizon LLM agents: failed trajectories may contain correct steps, while successful trajectories can contain errors. Trajectory-level skills can also shift teacher preferences at unrelated steps, introducing off-target distillation signals. We introduce SCOPE, a framework for Selective Critical-Step On-Policy Self-Distillation. After a supervised warm-up, the policy analyzes its own trajectories, generates step-specific hindsight skills, and distills their guidance only at selected error steps. A bounded signed teacher-student coefficient stabilizes auxiliary updates while preserving positive reinforcement and negative correction. A success-relative pairwise length reward further encourages efficient task completion. Experiments on ALFWorld and WebShop demonstrate consistent improvements across multiple model backbones. With Qwen2.5-3B, SCOPE achieves 97.7% success on ALFWorld and 85.2% on WebShop, outperforming SEED by 5.9 and 6.3 percentage points, respectively. On unseen ALFWorld tasks, SCOPE reaches 89.6% success, improving over GRPO by 23.2 percentage points with 46.7% fewer interactions. Scaling to Qwen3-32B, SCOPE achieves 99.2% success on seen ALFWorld tasks and 94.0% on unseen tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3095044fac305f7d399eb635c18a3280b7b9d7e2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38352", "CorpusId": 295371908}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3095044fac305f7d399eb635c18a3280b7b9d7e2", "title": "WeVisCut: The Whole Editing Room in One Agent From Narration to Long-Form Video", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38352, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2355200232", "name": "Chen-Yu Mu"}, {"authorId": "2107017232", "name": "Wen-Ming Weng"}, {"authorId": "2524836981", "name": "Qin-Rui Wang"}, {"authorId": "2333914032", "name": "Xing-Song Ye"}, {"authorId": "2349398803", "name": "Hao Yu"}, {"authorId": "2260828704", "name": "Jun He"}, {"authorId": "2372153050", "name": "Chong Sun"}, {"authorId": "2334702567", "name": "Chen Li"}, {"authorId": "2394072431", "name": "Jing Lyu"}, {"authorId": "3444345", "name": "Er-Kun Yang"}, {"authorId": "2375161305", "name": "Cheng Deng"}], "abstract": "Producing long-form videos from narration requires interdependent decisions that remain coherent across shots. Existing agentic editors often use multi-stage workflows driven by general models, leaving the quality-cost trade-off of compact, task-adapted policies unclear. We address this gap with WeVisCut, a long-form script-to-video system that connects tool execution with policy learning. Its agent harness maintains shared production state so that tool feedback informs subsequent decisions and repairs. Because finished videos do not expose these decisions, our four-stage data pipeline combines editing inversion with verified replay to turn recovered editing intent into executable supervision. Applied to 3,750 finished videos, this pipeline yields over 130K training examples. We use this supervision to train a single 9B policy that coordinates production and recovers from failures within the same harness. Moreover, we introduce a 100-task benchmark and five metrics assessing editing quality and delivery reliability. Under a shared tool environment, WeVisCut substantially outperforms its base model and achieves comparable performance to Claude Opus 5 while using nearly 33x fewer generated tokens per successful output minute. It also outperforms task-matched native editors on our proposed metrics."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6b354058e25ab0184a8cf84934bc09faaa8fdde7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36660", "CorpusId": 294524268}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6b354058e25ab0184a8cf84934bc09faaa8fdde7", "title": "The View from Nowhere: Population-Centered Agent Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36660, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2521792727", "name": "Shuo Yan"}, {"authorId": "2521582669", "name": "Ning-Ning Hou"}, {"authorId": "2326915783", "name": "Hao-Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2374041291", "name": "Hao-Chen Pei"}, {"authorId": "2520331106", "name": "Shang Gao"}], "abstract": "Self-improving AI systems can revise the designs that govern their own behavior. Yet existing approaches center improvement on selected parent agents and their evolutionary experience, leaving the broader population largely underused. We identify a substantial gap between population coverage (i.e., the tasks solved by at least one evolved variant) and the performance of the best individual agent. This gap shows that useful capabilities and diagnostic evidence emerge across the population without being consolidated into its leading agents. These observations motivate the view from nowhere, a principle for agent evolution: an agent should be improved by interpreting its behavior through population-wide patterns of success, failure, and prior self-modification. We instantiate this principle as Population-Centered Evolution (PCE). PCE distils task records and modification histories from the population into a population block, which captures recurring failures, complementary successes, and the effects of previously attempted modifications to guide improvements to a selected parent's harness. Under matched models, initial harnesses, and evolution schedules, PCE achieves 72.1% success on SWE-bench Verified and 96.7% on Polyglot, improving over a strong group-evolution baseline by 3.2 and 5.9 percentage points. In our evaluation, PCE reduces the gap between population coverage and best-agent performance by one-third on SWE-bench and closes it on Polyglot. These results suggest that treating the population as evidence, not merely as candidates for selection, provides a complementary principle for agent evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1639722492ef3f5ecb792f60ecbcc609edc7086b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36848", "CorpusId": 294621789}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1639722492ef3f5ecb792f60ecbcc609edc7086b", "title": "Evolving Agent Teams", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36848, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2265490669", "name": "Shi-Yi Cao"}, {"authorId": "2297406205", "name": "Zi-Ming Mao"}, {"authorId": "2265517766", "name": "Da-Cheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2308794329", "name": "Joseph Gonzalez"}, {"authorId": "2316149874", "name": "Ion Stoica"}], "abstract": "The rapid progress of software engineering agents drives interest in applying them to open-ended problems such as GPU kernel optimization. However, we find that existing coding agents converge to local optima on real-world, expert-written kernels (e.g., FlashInfer). We introduce Evolving Agent Teams, a multi-agent framework that escapes such local optima. It features (1) a central supervisor that diagnoses each local optimum and restructures a team of agents; and (2) a persistent file system that comprises a learning document, an evolution history, and per-team artifacts, grounding restructuring with accumulated experience. Under a matched token budget, Evolving Agent Teams consistently outperforms strong coding-agent baselines. With an extended token budget, Evolving Agent Teams generates a CUDA kernel that reaches 1.2x mean speedup over the production FlashInfer MLA paged decode kernel from scratch and wins on 36 of 47 production workloads from FlashInfer-Bench (0.86-0.99x on the remaining 11). It also demonstrates superior performance on tasks such as KDA prefill on AMD MI350X (3.01x over vLLM) and NVIDIA B200 (3.44x over FlashKDA), MoE expert-offload serving on Apple silicon (30.3 tokens/s decode for Qwen3.8-Flash-Next in MLX 4-bit on a 48 GB M4 Pro), and RoboDojo robot manipulation (62.1 on held-out seeds vs. 55.3 for the best leaderboard entry). Code is available at https://github.com/caoshiyi/EAT."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "34c78f3212282c713aab35598c4057834468a8d7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.37935", "CorpusId": 295319266}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/34c78f3212282c713aab35598c4057834468a8d7", "title": "SkillMem: Compact External Skill Memory for Latent Multi-Agent Reasoning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37935, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2353215830", "name": "Yuxuan Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2343636956", "name": "Mohammad Mohammadi Amiri"}, {"authorId": "2292347619", "name": "Pin-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "3377711", "name": "K. Murugesan"}], "abstract": "Latent multi-agent systems reduce inference cost by keeping intermediate reasoning and inter-agent communication in continuous representations.\\ Reusable skills, however, are commonly supplied as text or stored as layer-wise Key-Value states or skill-specific model parameters, introducing repeated processing or substantial per-skill storage.\\ We introduce \\textbf{SkillMem}, which represents each reusable skill as a small external latent memory and makes it directly available during latent multi-agent reasoning.\\ A role-conditioned cross-attention reader allows the same memory to guide different agents according to their roles and current hidden states.\\ We train the skill memories and shared reader through behavioral distillation, matching the change in token predictions induced by a textual skill while keeping the backbone frozen.\\ For our experiments, we obtain textual skills from paired successful and failed multi-agent trajectories, although existing skills can also be compiled directly.\\ Across six benchmarks and Qwen3 models ranging from 4B to 14B parameters, SkillMem provides strong gains for smaller backbones while preserving efficient latent multi-agent inference across model scales.\\ We use the same $8\\times256$ memory configuration across all evaluated model scales, requiring 9.6\\,KB of skill-specific storage per skill, including its retrieval embedding.\\ For a library of 100 skills, SkillMem reduces total storage by more than $38\\times$ compared with alternative skill representations and achieves up to a $4.23\\times$ end-to-end inference speedup.\\ These results show that compact procedural memories can be reused without sacrificing the efficiency of latent multi-agent reasoning."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c567ebe6a83082ae825184ec8952d1cfd6241d74", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35616", "CorpusId": 294365082}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c567ebe6a83082ae825184ec8952d1cfd6241d74", "title": "Hierarchical Skill Evolution for Coding Agent", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35616, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2292481494", "name": "Jun Gao"}, {"authorId": "2333944473", "name": "Yun Peng"}, {"authorId": "2308067782", "name": "Chang-Hai Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2210201247", "name": "Yu-Han Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2294982159", "name": "Xiang-Yun Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2528426811", "name": "Shiyang Zhang Yuhua Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2302525910", "name": "Luo-Zheng Qin"}, {"authorId": "2335563748", "name": "Qian Qiao"}, {"authorId": "2349734078", "name": "Xi Ma"}, {"authorId": "2487308811", "name": "Bai-Cheng Shang"}, {"authorId": "2382482720", "name": "Zhen Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2053830598", "name": "Li-Zhi Liao"}, {"authorId": "2348190986", "name": "Xiao-Xue Ren"}], "abstract": "Skills provide agents with reusable experience to guide future decisions without modifying the underlying model. However, coding trajectories are often entangled with task- and repository-specific contexts, making it challenging to distill procedural skills that generalize across diverse coding environments. To address this challenge, we present Hierarchical Skill Evolution (HSE), which progressively distills procedural knowledge from coding trajectories through hierarchical evidence aggregation. HSE first extracts task-level evidence from individual trajectories, then aggregates recurring evidence within the same repository to form repository-specific skill candidates, and finally consolidates consistent patterns across repositories into transferable skills across repositories. The evolution is driven by an external writer-evaluator loop that generates and assesses skills while keeping the underlying coding model frozen. The loop externally updates the writer and evaluator policies to improve subsequent skill generation and assessment. This hierarchy progressively filters task-specific details while preserving procedures that generalize across diverse coding environments. Successful trajectories yield reusable guidance for future tasks, while failed trajectories reveal recurring failure modes that guide avoidance and recovery. We evaluate HSE on SWE-bench Verified and DeepSWE, using GLM-5.2 across Pi and Claude Code harnesses and GPT-5.5 and Opus 4.8 with Pi. Across both benchmarks, 10 skills evolved solely from SWE-Gym improve coding performance across all evaluated settings, demonstrating transfer across benchmarks, coding models, and agent harnesses. We further evolve the skill library from target-benchmark trajectories without verifier feedback, relying solely on the learned evaluator for candidate assessment and achieving additional gains through benchmark-tailored skills."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d51cba2213de0ec6e70d0f299f49af90d3c50a45", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35051", "CorpusId": 294365858}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d51cba2213de0ec6e70d0f299f49af90d3c50a45", "title": "VISTA: Active Exploration for Self-Improvement in Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35051, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2356828633", "name": "Zhang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2293868235", "name": "Sheng-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2228313450", "name": "Si-Min Huang"}, {"authorId": "2145944822", "name": "Min Chen"}, {"authorId": "2257004568", "name": "Chong Xia"}, {"authorId": "2370933013", "name": "Xin Fei"}, {"authorId": "2295741834", "name": "Yue-Qi Duan"}], "abstract": "Agentic embodied intelligence plays a foundational role in open-ended, dynamic physical environments. Rather than treating policy learning as an end-to-end mapping from instructions to actions, recent studies reveal that embodied systems depend not only on pre-trained foundation models, but also on harness. Prior approaches largely treat rollouts as static logs for post-hoc reflection, producing harness updates without autonomously testing and refining them through subsequent embodied interaction. To address this challenge, we propose VISTA, a meta-agent framework that transforms post-hoc rollout reflection into active self-exploration for embodied harness improvement. Specifically, VISTA maintains a harness graph that records the lineage of proposed harnesses, which enables the meta-agent to retrieve prior hypotheses, build on effective mechanisms, and avoid repeatedly exploring failed directions. Each iteration begins from a retained harness version and its execution evidence. Rather than directly committing an update from these historical trajectories, the meta-agent actively explores in the environment. It autonomously generates new rollouts, probes execution failures and recovery behaviors, and iteratively modifies the harness based on the resulting evidence. The refined harness is then evaluated afresh and added to the harness graph. We evaluate VISTA on the LIBERO-Pro, RoboCasa, and RoboTwin benchmarks. Experimental results demonstrate that VISTA progressively improves task success and is more rollout-efficient than static reflection, achieving higher success rates with fewer simulator rollouts."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "70a22ae04e66a8b60f6ddd586e34a4c3585634b6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35604", "CorpusId": 294365333}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/70a22ae04e66a8b60f6ddd586e34a4c3585634b6", "title": "RoboDoctor: Training-Free Recursive Self-Improvement for Robot Policies", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35604, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2257104073", "name": "He-Yang Yu"}, {"authorId": "2523157042", "name": "Hao-Min Li"}, {"authorId": "2524436630", "name": "Si-Hang Li"}, {"authorId": "2458315725", "name": "Ji-Xin Yan"}, {"authorId": "2519439193", "name": "Shi-Yun Peng"}, {"authorId": "2266437328", "name": "Wan-Qi Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2355356049", "name": "Tian-Yu Xin"}, {"authorId": "2521177907", "name": "Yuan Yao"}, {"authorId": "2149326880", "name": "Xiao-Yu Tian"}, {"authorId": "2494018942", "name": "Yi-Ming Li"}], "abstract": "Generalist robot policies and language-model agents provide complementary capabilities, but completing a task also requires adapting their use to the consequences of physical interaction. We study execution as a locus of adaptation: feedback guides the current action and supplies evidence for revising subsequent decisions under fixed model weights. We introduce RoboDoctor, a training-free framework that connects diagnosis, grounded action, and persistent experience across three revision targets. The harness allocates control between policy and agent; intervention determines when to assist while preserving useful policy behavior; and operation refines physical methods through observed effects. Linked experience records retain the reason for a decision, the attempted method, its outcome, and conditions for reuse. On a curated archive of 54 RoboDojo-Sim task entries, RoboDoctor achieves a five-dimension mean score of 57.10, with a native success rate of 49.44%. Recorded interactions illustrate strategy changes, selective correction, and grasp revision. These findings characterize execution-time adaptation in the evaluated system; controlled trials remain necessary to isolate the contribution of cross-episode experience."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0052c6c5e3f96370d2d57d551a2347bf99de7bfa", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34977", "CorpusId": 294366331}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0052c6c5e3f96370d2d57d551a2347bf99de7bfa", "title": "Adaptive Memory Index Evolution for Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34977, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2257349205", "name": "Yun-Tong Hu"}, {"authorId": "2204144046", "name": "Tong-Li Su"}, {"authorId": "2342458025", "name": "Yuan-Fu Sun"}, {"authorId": "2447341271", "name": "Shuo-Tian Ma"}, {"authorId": "2282981308", "name": "Qiao-Yu Tan"}, {"authorId": "2377292005", "name": "Liang Zhao"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon agents continually accumulate experience, yet retaining a memory does not guarantee access when it is needed. Existing systems can adapt both memory content and access; we isolate persistent access updates as a separately controlled adaptation layer. We formulate this target as Memory Index Evolution and instantiate it with Adaptive Memory Index Evolution (AMIE), a lightweight plug-in layer that evolves per-memory access state from deployment retrievals without modifying stored content or retraining the host memory system. AMIE distils a semantic teacher's keep/suppress judgments into a retrieval-conditioned policy and accumulates its selective actions into bounded state that reweights subsequent host retrievals. Surprisingly, even a zero-call policy that simply accumulates retrieval frequency can match or outperform semantic policies in several settings, revealing retrieval traffic itself as a strong signal for access adaptation, while learned selectivity improves robustness when frequently retrieved memories remain useful. Across five retrieval-based hosts and three benchmarks, trained AMIE improves downstream F1 or accuracy in all 15 comparisons, by 0.70--3.37 percentage points (up to 25% relative), and improves retrieval in all 10 comparisons. Controlled analyses show that persistent competitor suppression can expose previously unseen evidence, while suppressing repeatedly useful memories creates the complementary failure mode. Together, these results establish persistent memory access as a practical adaptation layer, distinct from the content it serves, in retrieval-based agent memory systems."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "24afe3b5b2a10d92b3aa9ecb57ffa09c40d55366", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35013", "CorpusId": 294366415}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/24afe3b5b2a10d92b3aa9ecb57ffa09c40d55366", "title": "Multiscale Policy Optimization for Long-Horizon LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35013, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2283841222", "name": "Tao Ren"}, {"authorId": "2156108232", "name": "Hui Yang"}, {"authorId": "2326669139", "name": "Jia Deng"}, {"authorId": "2267161179", "name": "Ting-Yang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2307111273", "name": "Qi-Yao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2283546526", "name": "Shuo Tang"}, {"authorId": "2349075584", "name": "Ziyang Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2218318364", "name": "Yu-Hua Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2479172209", "name": "Guang-Hao Li"}, {"authorId": "2334893634", "name": "Hua-Tong Song"}, {"authorId": "2511770988", "name": "Yi-Ning Li"}, {"authorId": "2257392203", "name": "Yi-Fan Luo"}, {"authorId": "2494836585", "name": "Kun Ding"}, {"authorId": "2344833453", "name": "Feng-Jie Chang"}, {"authorId": "2398802789", "name": "Chuan Hao"}, {"authorId": "2384120505", "name": "Ran Tao"}, {"authorId": "2346322341", "name": "Bryan Dai"}, {"authorId": "2265751832", "name": "Jia-Feng Liang"}, {"authorId": "1491631680", "name": "Jinyang Jiang"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon LLM agents interact with their environments through complete assistant turns while generating each action token by token. We observe that critic values vary primarily across turns, motivating optimization anchored at turns with finer credit allocation among tokens. We propose Multiscale Policy Optimization (MPO), combining Multiscale Advantage Estimation (MAE) and turn-level importance sampling to align credit assignment and policy updates at the turn scale. Extensive experiments across search, tool-use, and automation tasks with 4B and 30B-A3B models demonstrate consistent gains over strong RL baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a1feaca240637e83e164fe78ba182d985dbee711", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36143", "CorpusId": 294523726}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a1feaca240637e83e164fe78ba182d985dbee711", "title": "Program-Level Adversarial Co-Adaptive Self-Play for Robotic Security", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36143, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2382767915", "name": "Leo Benaharon"}, {"authorId": "2500254589", "name": "Chi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2524299372", "name": "Wei Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2349476777", "name": "Wei-Fan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2381984843", "name": "Yi-Fan Wei"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Yong Sun"}], "abstract": "Coding agents can adapt robotic software by revising executable programs, but successful responses to an evolving opponent may not remain effective. We present a framework for program-level adversarial co-adaptive self-play in which coding agents generate attacks and defenses from tested opponent code and mission feedback, without model fine-tuning. Environment-specific interfaces support external programs for autonomous driving and SDK-level defenses for humanoid locomotion. Two eight-generation case studies reveal concrete software responses: a driving defender introduces direct-actuation recovery, while a humanoid defender revises incoming-state validation. These responses provide local task recovery but do not establish sustained robustness. The framework makes such adaptation and limitations observable through executable artifacts and task-level evidence."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "adf24071fc463072f790918451c1cdac498b1fa8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33956", "CorpusId": 294367022}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/adf24071fc463072f790918451c1cdac498b1fa8", "title": "Continual Learning Exam: Benchmarking Long-Horizon RSI and Experiential Learning in LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33956, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2508880707", "name": "Hao-Yu Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2306396908", "name": "Si-Tian Tu"}, {"authorId": "2348490567", "name": "Yuxuan Huang"}, {"authorId": "2375284697", "name": "Hui-Chi Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2155273086", "name": "Zhangyue Yin"}, {"authorId": "2520542265", "name": "Jia-Hao Huang"}, {"authorId": "14745697", "name": "Jing-Yuan Xu"}, {"authorId": "2520023722", "name": "Tian-Miao Shi"}, {"authorId": "2510696762", "name": "Jin-Song Shi"}, {"authorId": "2522084696", "name": "Nian Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2479579929", "name": "Yangzhihao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2520141130", "name": "Zhuo-Lang Chan"}, {"authorId": "2520106324", "name": "Hui-Yan Hu"}, {"authorId": "2446444133", "name": "Chen-Chao Sheng"}, {"authorId": "2112601905", "name": "Ya-Qing Luo"}, {"authorId": "2487644709", "name": "Yue Chen"}, {"authorId": "2520118688", "name": "Hua-Xiu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2325905652", "name": "Meng Fang"}, {"authorId": "2257572528", "name": "Jun Wang"}], "abstract": "Continual learning is the ability of LLM agents to improve over time by acquiring, refining, and applying knowledge from experience while retaining useful prior knowledge. It is essential for agents learning over long time horizons, yet current evaluations largely assess individual task performance rather than improvement through accumulated experience. We introduce Continual Learning Exam (CLE), a large-scale benchmark developed by 50+ domain experts to measure experience-driven improvement in realistic professional workflows. CLE comprises 120 human-authored tasks, each organised as a five-level ladder, yielding 600 levels spanning 89 professional roles across 12 occupational domains. Agents must pass each level to unlock the next, with later levels building on experience accumulated during earlier assignments. An evaluation agent assesses each submission against human-authored rubrics. We compare a single-submission setting with one that provides diagnostic feedback after failure and allows one revision. Across 17 harness-model configurations using frontier LLMs, feedback and revision yield a median completion gain of 5.3 percentage points. Yet the best-performing system clears only 47.7% of levels and completes all five levels in just 23.3% of tasks, highlighting the difficulty of sustained learning from experience for existing frontier LLMs. As such, the dataset can also be used to post-train LLMs to strengthen their continual learning capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "730b21e7e7a3210498573684e58412ff58bbc2d3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34238", "CorpusId": 294364774}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/730b21e7e7a3210498573684e58412ff58bbc2d3", "title": "$\\Delta\\tau$-Bench: Pushing Agents Toward a Moving Capability", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34238, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2190751119", "name": "Shuo-Fei Qiao"}, {"authorId": "2387896316", "name": "Xuan-Guang Pan"}, {"authorId": "2340722387", "name": "Yijun Chen"}, {"authorId": "2386094453", "name": "Wen-Di Feng"}, {"authorId": "2187129580", "name": "Bin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2278840339", "name": "Run-Nan Fang"}, {"authorId": "2283182145", "name": "Yu-Qi Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2253472695", "name": "Shu-Min Deng"}, {"authorId": "2371990732", "name": "Emine Yilmaz"}], "abstract": "In this paper, we introduce $\\Delta\\tau$-Bench, an executable agent benchmark that models the structural evolution of large tool ecosystems. In $\\Delta\\tau$-Bench, independently evolving services continually reshape how tasks can be accomplished: familiar tools may disappear or be replaced, while more efficient alternatives emerge amid overlapping functionality and noisy documentation. Against this moving capability frontier, we evaluate whether agents can proactively discover efficient tool-use paths and promptly revise their accumulated procedural knowledge, bringing together demands that existing benchmarks fail to examine. $\\Delta\\tau$-Bench comprises 1,588 validated multi-turn tasks across 42 domains and six successive ecosystem states generated through capability-preserving transformations. Our evaluation shows that even the strongest evaluated model or harness achieves only 42.3% task success, while initial gains from existing skill-learning methods can not consistently translate into better adaptation as the ecosystem evolves. These findings motivate Steward, an ecosystem manager that jointly manages tools and procedural knowledge through incremental skill maintenance and dynamic provisioning during task execution. Experiments show that Steward sustains higher task success across ecosystem changes with lower skill-maintenance overhead, while improving both recovery from disrupted workflows and adoption of more efficient alternatives."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6234b3968ed05cb495a37eae16d133daf9effc84", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34043", "CorpusId": 294366264}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6234b3968ed05cb495a37eae16d133daf9effc84", "title": "Holding a Steady Course: Knowledge Evolution from Expertise and Lessons for Continual Agent Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34043, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2284912807", "name": "Wenzhe Fan"}, {"authorId": "2108813860", "name": "Benjamin Steenhoek"}, {"authorId": "2267452746", "name": "Gaurav Mittal"}, {"authorId": "2112337704", "name": "Yu Hu"}], "abstract": "Autonomous agents are increasingly deployed across sequences of related tasks, yet they often fail to retain and improve their knowledge over time. Existing approaches convert interaction trajectories into memories or reusable skills, but experience is not automatically reliable knowledge. Automatically collected skills and memories can propagate errors, mislead, and corrupt useful knowledge. To address this gap, we introduce Knowledge Evolution from Expertise and Lessons (KEEL), an agent-agnostic framework that uses verifier evidence to transform successful behavior into reusable skills and failures into corrective lessons. As new evidence accumulates, individual skill claims can be supported, contradicted, replaced, or retired. On SkillFlow benchmark, KEEL improves task success by 4.2 to 21.7 percentage points over the no-knowledge baseline, compared with gains of 2.4 to 9.6 points from SkillFlow's default method. Ablations show that skills and lessons are complementary. These results show that reliable continual agent learning depends not simply on accumulating experience, but on continually validating and evolving the knowledge derived from it. Our code is available at: https://github.com/microsoft/KEEL-TTCL26."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c113b04a15c1d4057d4b8f2777dd3f4b9e3a4f26", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34450", "CorpusId": 294367222}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c113b04a15c1d4057d4b8f2777dd3f4b9e3a4f26", "title": "EvolveMAS: A Unified Self-Evolution Framework for Decentralized Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34450, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2364789332", "name": "Ya-Lun Wu"}, {"authorId": "2357498174", "name": "Qi-Wei Ma"}, {"authorId": "2372232199", "name": "Bing-Bing Xu"}, {"authorId": "2266277013", "name": "Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2075398318", "name": "Long Bai"}, {"authorId": "2261851935", "name": "Bo-Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2259858493", "name": "Jia-Tong Li"}, {"authorId": "2382965039", "name": "Zhe-Qi Lv"}, {"authorId": "1429199889", "name": "Shao-Zu Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2118951553", "name": "Pei-Ying Wang"}, {"authorId": "2506267525", "name": "Jia-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2311074323", "name": "An Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2351809900", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}], "abstract": "Decentralized multi-agent systems (MAS) enable large language model agents to interact without centralized control. Agents need to adapt to other agents at test time and reuse accumulated experience to refine their interaction strategies for future interactions across cooperative, mixed-motive, and adversarial settings. However, existing approaches often develop adaptation mechanisms for particular interaction settings. This raises a central question: how can decentralized MAS support test-time adaptation and reliable experience reuse across interaction settings? We propose EvolveMAS, a unified self-evolution framework that supports experience-driven adaptation within and across episodes. EvolveMAS pairs each main agent with a dedicated MemoryAgent for test-time memory governance, which maintains isolated Structured Memory outside the main agent's reasoning context. EvolveMAS organizes adaptation into two complementary processes: Within-Episode Online Adaptation and Cross-Episode Knowledge Evolution. During Within-Episode Online Adaptation, MemoryAgent updates episodic, semantic, and working memory from interaction evidence to inform subsequent actions. During Cross-Episode Knowledge Evolution, the main agent uses memory and episode outcomes to distill reusable interaction knowledge comprising partner profiles and interaction strategies for subsequent episodes. Across five benchmarks and six evaluation configurations spanning the three interaction settings, EvolveMAS achieves the highest normalized average score of 77.2 among the compared methods. Ablations show that test-time memory governance and cross-episode knowledge reuse both improve performance. Interaction traces further illustrate how updated memory and distilled knowledge guide subsequent interactions. These results support experience-driven adaptation in decentralized MAS without updating model parameters."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "886a1998b96df61ee7459d584f766a54482d179d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34500", "CorpusId": 294366645}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/886a1998b96df61ee7459d584f766a54482d179d", "title": "CaliMem: Search-Efficient Data Agent via Calibrated Memory", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34500, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "1949334927", "name": "Han-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2329081678", "name": "Athan Z. Li"}, {"authorId": "2054842299", "name": "Fei-Yang Kang"}, {"authorId": "2130518057", "name": "Jia-Chen T. Wang"}, {"authorId": "2475498001", "name": "Yu-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "2357923277", "name": "Rui-Shan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2268321162", "name": "Ruo-Xi Jia"}], "abstract": "Data curation is a first-order design choice in machine learning, yet tailoring training data to a downstream model and task remains costly in labor, expertise, and compute. Datasets are therefore often processed through fixed pipelines and reused across applications. We show that this assumption is fragile: across 55 dataset--model tasks spanning biological domains, the same curation recipe can help one model while hurting another. Effective curation must adapt to the downstream setting, motivating automated search over data recipes. We first build an agentic system that searches for curation policies at deployment, improving data utility via \\textit{test-time curation (TTC)}. Although achieving performance gains, its search is driven largely by recent feedback and remains local. Adding persistent memory helps the agent retain evidence across iterations and reveals a deeper problem: it forms claims that extend beyond the conditions in which they were observed, sometimes abandoning an entire strategy after a single unsuccessful trial. To address this problem, we introduce CaliMem, a calibrated-memory agentic loop that requires the agent to predict the outcome of each proposed policy before executing it. Comparing this prediction with the observed result gives the agent a \\textit{reality check} on the claims behind its proposal. It uses the prediction error to retain supported claims, revise mistaken ones, and specify the conditions under which a conclusion holds. This makes the agent's beliefs testable, reduces overgeneralization, and supports more effective long-horizon exploration. Across biological and LLM post-training tasks, CaliMem consistently outperforms fixed curation baselines and published methods, and exceeds full-data training by up to 19.4\\%. Code is open-sourced."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "62712cd8621cb7ed0a4fb0370c91f8316dd28544", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03685", "CorpusId": 295507384}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/62712cd8621cb7ed0a4fb0370c91f8316dd28544", "title": "LOLPlay: Benchmarking Real-Time Visual Agents in League of Legends", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03685, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2302532793", "name": "Zhao-Jian Yu"}], "abstract": "Visual agents need to coordinate reasoning and action while their environment evolves during inference. We introduce LOLPlay, a benchmark in native \\emph{League of Legends} with 36 champion-specific tasks spanning resource management, combat, cooperation, and objectives. Agents interact through screenshots and mouse--keyboard actions; automated initialization and 82 executable subgoal checks distinguish partial progress from completion. Across 324 task evaluations of three models at three reasoning-effort settings, GPT-6 Astra achieves the highest task success rate of 25.0\\% at low and medium effort, and the highest subgoal success rate of 40.3\\% at medium effort. However, high effort improves neither metric over low effort for any model. For Astra, longer requests leave fewer interaction rounds, suggesting a cost to additional reasoning in real-time control. Trajectories reveal incomplete subgoal composition and actions executing after the observed scene changes, motivating agents that combine visual reasoning with timely action."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bc1306df633b8067cea2f628202d8bbf58af2fdf", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33487", "CorpusId": 294227692}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bc1306df633b8067cea2f628202d8bbf58af2fdf", "title": "ReSight: Task-Driven Self-Improving Memory for Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33487, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2423412076", "name": "Li-Rong Che"}, {"authorId": "2473776641", "name": "Yang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2486727700", "name": "Yi-Meng Su"}, {"authorId": "34776599", "name": "Da-Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2354550156", "name": "Zhen-Yu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2507855939", "name": "Ji-Aan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2316673843", "name": "Yuzhe Yang"}, {"authorId": "2467100130", "name": "Xu Cao"}, {"authorId": "2345700914", "name": "Chuang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2430939627", "name": "Jiang-Feng Su"}, {"authorId": "2329880419", "name": "Xue-Qian Wang"}], "abstract": "Robots can use historical observations to answer spatiotemporal queries in familiar environments, but the presence of relevant evidence does not imply that existing tools support the operations a task requires. We introduce ReSight, a task-driven self-improving memory system for embodied agents that expands the capabilities for processing and accessing historical observations as task demands arise. ReSight retains keyframes and their spatiotemporal associations, separating observation evidence from extensible derived fields so that stored observations can be reprocessed for new needs. An execution agent and a coding agent share this memory through different interfaces: the former calls registered tools within a bounded online budget, while the latter uses a programmable workspace and a larger exploration budget to inspect evidence, write programs, and acquire models as needed. To realize capability expansion in ReSight, we develop Explore-Distill-Mount (EDM), which identifies capability gaps from execution and exploration records without task reference answers and converts exploration-derived solutions into reusable memory builders, query tools, and skills, extending memory representations and access capabilities as needed. Experiments on three public benchmarks demonstrate effective base memory access and capability expansion. With the execution model and online budget held fixed within each benchmark, EDM reduces mean position error on OC-NaVQA from 33.10 m to 24.72 m and improves high-level success on FindingDory from 38.3% to 63.8%. With the tools and skill frozen, success still improves by 28.3 percentage points on FindingDory episodes excluded from exploration and distillation. Experiments on real robot patrol memories further demonstrate reuse of these capabilities for subsequent queries and new memories, with fewer online tool calls on average."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "de0794fbfc9197c0fcde9b9a304e5fb8637ec6af", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33690", "CorpusId": 294227947}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/de0794fbfc9197c0fcde9b9a304e5fb8637ec6af", "title": "PACE: Research Proving Agent for Adaptive Conjecture Exploration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33690, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2484755943", "name": "Shu-Rong Miao"}, {"authorId": "2505395222", "name": "Fei-Ming Wang"}, {"authorId": "2346447046", "name": "Wen-Tao Long"}, {"authorId": "2292085498", "name": "Chen-Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2365041040", "name": "Kun Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2242221741", "name": "Zai-Wen Wen"}], "abstract": "Many mathematical research agents focus on solving problems with fixed statements. However, conjecture research, especially in applied mathematics, may require revising candidate questions or conclusions as investigation proceeds, coupling problem formulation with proof development. We present PACE, a research-proof agent that supports this process through a structured research loop that selects mathematical objectives and develops or challenges candidate mechanisms. The whole prover constructs proofs from these mechanisms, and an independent verifier checks them against the original target. Evidence-tracking memory stores assumptions and dependencies arising during research and marks evidence for revalidation when a candidate or condition changes. This memory supports task-specific context composition that keeps checked premises, exploratory material and unresolved obligations distinct. To evaluate proof construction and conjecture research, we introduce ResearchProofBench, an applied-mathematics benchmark focused on optimization, comprising 51 known-proof tasks with 331 hidden checkpoints under historical abstract access and six open-problem cases. The known-proof tasks assess reconstruction of published results, while the open-problem cases assess mathematical investigation and proof development. On the 51 known-proof tasks, PACE achieves an aggregate Proof Score of 75.16 and completes 20 proofs. In conjecture research, PACE develops conditional results, with its submissions receiving higher review scores than the compared baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c76b65d2f5acbd8e97aa15b1b859f0a7dd84ed55", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33396", "CorpusId": 294228091}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c76b65d2f5acbd8e97aa15b1b859f0a7dd84ed55", "title": "Fast Instinct, Slow Reason: A Dual-Speed Agentic Framework for Self-Configuring Multi-Object Tracking in Satellite Video", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33396, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2403017878", "name": "Fan He"}, {"authorId": "2433738369", "name": "Bai-Ning Wu"}, {"authorId": "2362525915", "name": "Yan Li"}, {"authorId": "2272681609", "name": "Xiao-Yang Zeng"}], "abstract": "Multi-object tracking in satellite video underpins persistent monitoring, yet offline-tuned trackers lack the onboard intelligence and efficiency to adapt within an observation of tens of seconds. The root is that adaptation still means relabelling and retraining the detector, which cannot run on board in time. Tuning the tracker configuration strongly controls accuracy without touching the network, yet search needs labelled or proxy evaluations, and a language agent costs seconds and tokens per decision. We propose a dual-speed agentic framework pairing a fast instinct compiled offline with a slow reason serving the mission. Its algorithm, Arbitration of Unlabelled Shortlists from Prior Experience, or AUSPEX, learns a covering shortlist and a label-free ranker from labelled source tasks and commits one configuration per sequence. Its system, a Cascaded Onboard Runtime with Verified Uplinked Skills, or CORVUS, cascades reflex, skill, and agent layers, so that a verified skill commits 2.5 s into each observation while a language agent serves natural- language missions. On SatMTB and SatVideoDT with three recent detectors, AUSPEX raises IDF1 by 2.84 to 2.94 points and MOTA by 3.21 to 3.29 points on average without target labels, balances accuracy and labels best among sixteen methods, and loses at most 0.28 IDF1 points at 1.25 tracking passes. CORVUS executes four missions with execution accuracy up to 0.99 and target recall at the perception ceiling, spends no token on the routine decision, runs on Jetson Xavier NX, and hosts fifteen language models from six families without altering any commitment, demonstrating its extensibility and generalization."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6797ae04494ec47ca10899546c8bdd8fab5f5af1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33861", "CorpusId": 294365962}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6797ae04494ec47ca10899546c8bdd8fab5f5af1", "title": "ReGE: Stronger Agents through Experience Synthesis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33861, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2388706461", "name": "Jia-Rong Liang"}, {"authorId": "2238120100", "name": "Yubo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2342746625", "name": "Zi-Jie Liu"}, {"authorId": "2191206519", "name": "Yuxuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2343748885", "name": "Ping Nie"}, {"authorId": "2343777493", "name": "Wen-Hu Chen"}], "abstract": "Repeated attempts at interactive tasks uncover facts, useful operations, and failures that can inform subsequent execution. We introduce ReGE, a framework for turning this recorded experience into guidance for a fresh attempt. Its main implementation, ReGE-Syn, uses a single coach call to jointly synthesize bounded execution records and available artifacts into self-contained guidance, connecting task-specific findings with proposed actions and relevant pitfalls. A student starts from the task's initial environment state and adapts this guidance to current observations. Source generation, synthesis, and student execution use the same base model, without parameter updates or a stronger teacher. Across four benchmarks, ReGE improves accuracy by an average of 9.9 percentage points for GPT and 8.8 percentage points for Claude compared with unguided execution. On matched BrowseComp-Plus subsets, ReGE-Syn reduces guidance-construction tokens by 98.2\\% on GPT and 95.5\\% on Claude relative to adapted AggAgent, with observed success. Combined construction and execution tokens decrease by 15.7\\% and 35.4\\%, respectively, counting input plus output and excluding source generation. BrowseComp-Plus ablations further associate access to process information with fewer student steps and show that joint synthesis produces substantially shorter guidance than Direct history or Independent summaries, with observed success. These results demonstrate how agents can use their own recorded experience to improve subsequent task completion, and suggest a low-cost way to consolidate the execution records of parallel agent systems such as agent swarms."}}]}
